WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:05.638
- OK, a lot of season left, but are you
 the MVP?
- Me the MVP?
- Mmhmm.

00:00:06.706 --> 00:00:08.008
- Yeah, I came in this season thinking

00:00:08.008 --> 00:00:09.542
I was the MVP.
♪♪♪

00:00:19.619 --> 00:00:23.656
- DeMar, what up?
- Tay, what's going on?
- Nothing, it's good to see you.

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:27.494
- Always, always.
- Now, you know, last time I saw you,
your hair was much shorter.

00:00:27.494 --> 00:00:28.862
- Yeah, yeah, yeah. It was a lot shorter.
Yeah.

00:00:28.862 --> 00:00:30.697
- So things have changed.
- Yeah.

00:00:30.697 --> 00:00:32.799
- Why did you decide to grow it out?

00:00:32.799 --> 00:00:34.134
- I don't know. I just got lazy.
- Yeah?

00:00:34.134 --> 00:00:35.535
- Just stopped getting a haircut.

00:00:35.535 --> 00:00:38.271
Just let it grow and…

00:00:38.271 --> 00:00:39.439
Just really just let it grow.

00:00:39.439 --> 00:00:42.075
- Yeah.
- Honestly.
- It's funny because that's
 how all these things happen.

00:00:42.108 --> 00:00:44.944
- Yeah, yeah.
- It's, like, either you couldn't
 find a good barber,
- Mmhmm.

00:00:45.245 --> 00:00:48.348
- You wanted to get the braids.
- Yeah.
- Or you're like, "I'm just done dealing with this, so

00:00:48.415 --> 00:00:50.250
I'm gonna grow it out."
- Just let it, let it go.

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:51.284
- Yeah. It looks good, though.

00:00:51.284 --> 00:00:52.619
- Then I started liking it, so I kept it, so.

00:00:52.619 --> 00:00:56.389
- Yeah, so you found a good braider
 in Chicago?
- Yeah.

00:00:56.689 --> 00:00:58.925
- OK.
- I got—yeah. Or sometimes I'll fly

00:00:59.993 --> 00:01:03.696
who does my hair from home just to come
braid my hair.
- Oh. Must be nice.

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:05.832
- I know it sounds bougie, but yeah.
- [Laughing]

00:01:06.533 --> 00:01:08.435
Oh, no, no, no. You gotta do what you
gotta do.

00:01:08.435 --> 00:01:11.237
- Nah, yeah. I got to, got to.
- To have the right person—
- You got to.
- Doing your hair.

00:01:12.472 --> 00:01:15.508
I'm really happy that I got to see you,
though, for myriad reasons.

00:01:15.508 --> 00:01:18.645
But one thing is I have a bone to pick with you.

00:01:19.646 --> 00:01:22.949
So on my show that we did,
what, like, two years ago,
- It was like, yeah…

00:01:22.949 --> 00:01:25.952
- Two-and-a-half years ago?
 You revealed that you rap.
- [Laughing] Yeah.

00:01:27.554 --> 00:01:30.523
- And you told me you was gonna
give me the song

00:01:30.523 --> 00:01:33.593
when you decided to put a song out.
- I did. You right. You right. You right.

00:01:33.827 --> 00:01:34.928
- The song is out.

00:01:34.928 --> 00:01:39.299
- Yeah.
- What happened?
- I put—it was, that song was such a, like,

00:01:40.667 --> 00:01:44.938
in the moment, like, my man Problem,
one of my close friends that rap,

00:01:45.271 --> 00:01:49.075
we was in Vail, Colorado,
and it was, like, 2:00 in the morning.

00:01:49.309 --> 00:01:53.513
And he was just, like, "Let's record a song
 and put it out tonight."

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:55.882
And we literally recorded a song

00:01:55.882 --> 00:01:59.352
and put it out that night,
like, in real time. Like…
- Yeah.

00:01:59.486 --> 00:02:02.555
- And I didn't think nothing about it.
That was my only chance to put a song out.
- OK.

00:02:02.822 --> 00:02:06.326
- It was in that moment, you know what
 I mean?
- Yeah.
- I just put it out, and it was cool. It was…

00:02:06.559 --> 00:02:07.460
- So it was a small window.

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:10.196
And if you didn't do it—
- Yeah, yeah. If I didn't
 do it then, it wasn't gonna happen.

00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:12.265
- Yeah, but still—
- Wasn't gonna happen.
- Only one song's out.

00:02:12.499 --> 00:02:14.901
- Yeah, it's only one song.
And what's crazy,

00:02:14.901 --> 00:02:16.769
we have a video to it too. Like—

00:02:18.004 --> 00:02:19.639
- Is that coming out?

00:02:19.639 --> 00:02:21.508
- I'm thinking about it. I'm
 thinking about putting it out.

00:02:21.508 --> 00:02:24.811
- What would sway you
to, "Yes, it's coming out"?
- I don't know.

00:02:24.811 --> 00:02:27.981
I gotta be in the, I gotta find
that same moment I had that night.

00:02:28.014 --> 00:02:30.884
- Yeah.
- I gotta find it one of these days,
 and I'll put it out. But we got one.

00:02:30.984 --> 00:02:34.888
- You know, we had Lou Williams on the show
 last season, and he said the same thing

00:02:34.888 --> 00:02:35.822
about having music.

00:02:35.822 --> 00:02:36.956
He's like, "No, if I wake up

00:02:36.956 --> 00:02:40.260
in the mood, sure."
- Yeah.
- "But I'm very rarely in the mood."
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:02:40.827 --> 00:02:44.731
- "But when it comes, I'll decide to,
 you know, bless you all—"
- Yep, yep.
- "With this music."

00:02:44.764 --> 00:02:46.466
What was the feedback on that song?

00:02:46.466 --> 00:02:48.401
- It was great. I mean, it was a lot of surprises

00:02:48.401 --> 00:02:52.972
because people didn't expect me to ever
put a song out or hear me on a song.

00:02:52.972 --> 00:02:55.341
And for the most part, it was dope.

00:02:55.341 --> 00:02:58.878
You know, some of my closest friends
that do know that I rap

00:02:59.445 --> 00:03:01.915
called me and was like,
didn't believe I even put out a song.

00:03:01.915 --> 00:03:04.050
But even though they hear all my songs,

00:03:04.384 --> 00:03:06.119
they were just shocked
that I put a song out,

00:03:06.119 --> 00:03:08.721
so it was cool.
- Yeah, any good feedback?

00:03:08.721 --> 00:03:09.622
I know when we spoke,

00:03:09.622 --> 00:03:12.792
you said, like, Kendrick Lamar has heard
your music,
- Yeah.
- Drake's heard your music.

00:03:12.825 --> 00:03:16.129
- Yeah.
- Did they hear the one you released?
- I'm pretty sure they did.

00:03:16.162 --> 00:03:18.531
I haven't heard no feedback for it.
- Oh, OK.

00:03:19.065 --> 00:03:19.966
- But, yeah, yeah.
- Hey, it was a good beat.

00:03:19.966 --> 00:03:21.501
I was jamming to it,

00:03:21.501 --> 00:03:23.803
you know?
- Yeah, it was just a moment.
It was a moment.
- Yes.
- Yeah.

00:03:24.204 --> 00:03:27.473
- But I say all that to say,
 like, time moves so quickly.
- Fast.

00:03:27.807 --> 00:03:31.844
- So much has changed
 from when I last saw you to now.

00:03:32.478 --> 00:03:35.215
So if you look back to that moment, right,
it was, you were playing

00:03:35.215 --> 00:03:38.384
with the Spurs.
- Mmhmm.
- Raptors in the Eastern Conference Finals.

00:03:38.751 --> 00:03:40.353
We were just talking about life—
- Mmhmm.

00:03:40.353 --> 00:03:42.455
- And everything you had been
through up to that point.

00:03:42.455 --> 00:03:44.090
If you think about that moment

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:47.961
and if you titled kind of that season
of your life, what would you call that?

00:03:47.961 --> 00:03:51.231
And what would you call this
one you're in now?
- Um, man,

00:03:52.565 --> 00:03:55.268
growing, you know?

00:03:55.268 --> 00:04:00.707
That moment then, you know, I was
in a tricky place mentally,

00:04:01.274 --> 00:04:06.379
emotionally, dealing with obviously
being traded, going to a new situation.

00:04:07.747 --> 00:04:11.651
Just, like, everything started to move
fast but slow down at the same time for me.

00:04:12.018 --> 00:04:14.287
For me, just kinda to lock in

00:04:15.221 --> 00:04:18.324
and kinda get out of my comfort zone
with basketball and with life

00:04:18.324 --> 00:04:22.662
and just try to figure out what direction
I wanted to grow in.

00:04:22.862 --> 00:04:25.999
You know, and that was kind of
the perspective I started to take from there

00:04:26.432 --> 00:04:29.802
and stopped looking at everything so negative
and just take everything

00:04:29.802 --> 00:04:32.138
in stride and learn from it
and grow from it

00:04:32.939 --> 00:04:35.875
from then on. And look

00:04:35.875 --> 00:04:36.643
where I'm at now, so.
- Absolutely. So

00:04:37.777 --> 00:04:39.979
that would be titled "growing."
- Yeah.

00:04:40.179 --> 00:04:44.217
- This is titled?
- [Sighing] This is titled…

00:04:46.753 --> 00:04:49.989
What they say, it's like…

00:04:49.989 --> 00:04:50.690
That's a good one.

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:53.826
I wish I would have
had some great comeback for that one.
- Lil' bar ready?

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:56.362
- Yeah, I think now it's just more so,
just,

00:04:56.929 --> 00:05:00.800
you know, growing and continuing
to manifest the things that, you know,

00:05:01.801 --> 00:05:05.738
that you want in life, you know what I
I mean? You know, we always try to

00:05:06.639 --> 00:05:08.708
strive for the success with, you know,

00:05:08.741 --> 00:05:11.511
things on the basketball court,
personal life, professional life.

00:05:11.978 --> 00:05:15.648
But just manifesting everything,
you know, a positive mindset.

00:05:16.115 --> 00:05:19.786
Mental approach to everything.
You know, it's tricky times we live in.

00:05:19.819 --> 00:05:22.255
You know, when we last talked,
you know, we weren't dealing with—

00:05:22.622 --> 00:05:23.856
- Yeah.
- What we dealing with

00:05:23.856 --> 00:05:27.327
now in the world, you know? And,
you know, just consistently

00:05:27.327 --> 00:05:30.029
trying to figure out
how to be that, you know, spark

00:05:30.730 --> 00:05:34.734
to the world that can help,
you know, with positivity

00:05:34.734 --> 00:05:36.836
more than anything.
I think we all need it,

00:05:36.836 --> 00:05:40.907
because it's very tricky times
right now.
- 100 percent.

00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:42.442
Well, I do have to say this about you.

00:05:42.442 --> 00:05:44.944
Any time someone says
anything about you ever,

00:05:44.944 --> 00:05:46.679
they talk about how nice and kind you are.
- Yeah.

00:05:46.679 --> 00:05:47.680
- I'm sure you know that.
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:52.018
- But you leave, like, a very good footprint with
everybody that you come in contact with.
- Yeah.

00:05:52.018 --> 00:05:55.355
- So, I'm sure all those things that you
wanna do in terms of being positive

00:05:55.355 --> 00:05:59.425
for people, you were already doing it.
And doing it now in Chicago.
- Yeah.

00:05:59.892 --> 00:06:03.329
- Which—thank you for bringing us here
because this is one of my favorite cities.

00:06:03.363 --> 00:06:06.466
You gotta get Harold's Chicken.
- Everything, yeah.
- You gotta get Sunda.

00:06:06.733 --> 00:06:08.201
You gotta get all the things.

00:06:08.201 --> 00:06:10.002
So, happy that you're here in Chicago now.

00:06:10.002 --> 00:06:12.271
Are you enjoying the city?
- Yeah, I love it.

00:06:14.474 --> 00:06:16.142
Can't even find the words at times.

00:06:16.142 --> 00:06:16.843
You know, it's such a

00:06:16.843 --> 00:06:20.980
great situation

00:06:21.314 --> 00:06:25.051
off and on the court.
You know, you're playing in Chicago.
- Mhmm.

00:06:26.018 --> 00:06:29.021
- One of the best sports cities in the world.

00:06:29.088 --> 00:06:33.459
You know, the culture, the history
behind it, everything that come with it.

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:38.998
So to be part of that, to be thriving
at a high level is an honor.

00:06:38.998 --> 00:06:41.033
It's definitely an honor.
And it's fun, you know?
- Yeah.

00:06:42.402 --> 00:06:45.371
- You can see it when we go out there and
 play as a team. For me personally,

00:06:45.371 --> 00:06:46.939
you know, it's obvious.

00:06:46.939 --> 00:06:49.308
- Yes, it's a huge, you know, sports city.

00:06:50.143 --> 00:06:53.379
But the Bulls, they haven't
been to the postseason since 2017.

00:06:53.679 --> 00:06:56.115
Haven't been to the Eastern Conference
Finals in 10 years.

00:06:56.549 --> 00:06:59.419
When you hear that, what do you think?
- I love the challenge.

00:06:59.819 --> 00:07:01.621
You know, that was my whole reasoning

00:07:01.621 --> 00:07:04.257
even coming here was to take
on all those challenges

00:07:04.257 --> 00:07:07.360
of what they haven't done,
what they couldn't do.

00:07:07.827 --> 00:07:09.162
Um, dealing with all the doubt.

00:07:09.162 --> 00:07:11.531
You know, you always wanna
take on challenges

00:07:11.531 --> 00:07:14.500
and prove the naysayers wrong.

00:07:14.667 --> 00:07:18.871
You know, and as a competitor, you know,
you want those things. You know, that was

00:07:18.871 --> 00:07:22.608
part of my motivation to come
here. Every guy on the team, motivation.

00:07:22.608 --> 00:07:25.411
So, you know, that's
definitely what we striving for.

00:07:25.411 --> 00:07:28.314
- When you looked at the Bulls,
 what are the things

00:07:28.314 --> 00:07:32.051
that you saw as challenges
and what are the things that you saw,

00:07:32.051 --> 00:07:34.520
"Oh, like, this is good.
I can work with this."

00:07:34.954 --> 00:07:35.988
- Um, the challenges was,

00:07:35.988 --> 00:07:39.759
you know, the culture is kind of,
you know, they haven't won, like you said,

00:07:39.759 --> 00:07:41.561
in a few years.

00:07:41.561 --> 00:07:44.330
They've been a team
that's, you know, been a lottery team, you know?

00:07:45.131 --> 00:07:47.567
You know, for me, I looked at the team,

00:07:47.567 --> 00:07:50.770
and me being a fan of
Zach LaVine his whole career,

00:07:51.737 --> 00:07:55.208
you know, looking at his career,
understanding what

00:07:55.208 --> 00:07:58.377
it's like to be in a moment that he was
in, not winning, wanting to win,

00:07:58.377 --> 00:08:00.413
wanting to get to the postseason
and being an All-Star.

00:08:00.847 --> 00:08:03.816
You know, not being appreciated
like he should with his talent

00:08:03.850 --> 00:08:06.419
that he have. We want to be
a part of that and help that.

00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:09.455
Help the organization, help the city.

00:08:09.455 --> 00:08:13.993
You know, I think to see—the league
thrives at a high level when a Chicago

00:08:14.260 --> 00:08:18.130
team is at the top of the league,
you know? And you see it now.

00:08:18.865 --> 00:08:20.967
And those are the challenges
and things that I looked at

00:08:21.801 --> 00:08:23.336
before I even make my decision.

00:08:23.336 --> 00:08:28.040
So, in the midst of me looking at it
that way, you know, you added Lonzo Ball,

00:08:28.441 --> 00:08:30.877
Alex Caruso, you add all these key players,

00:08:31.177 --> 00:08:34.514
Vucevic, to the team,
that made it even more intriguing.

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:37.450
- Yeah. Well, remember when everyone said,
"Hey, how are they—

00:08:37.450 --> 00:08:38.951
there's two ball-dominant guards. How's it gonna work?"
- Yeah.

00:08:38.951 --> 00:08:41.287
"Where are they going to get the defense from?"

00:08:41.921 --> 00:08:44.423
- Yeah. I love that. I hear it all, and I love it.

00:08:44.423 --> 00:08:48.394
You know, like I say, as a competitor,
you hear it and you

00:08:48.794 --> 00:08:50.096
laugh at it

00:08:50.096 --> 00:08:52.632
when you know the preparation that you putting in to prove

00:08:52.932 --> 00:08:53.900
everybody wrong.

00:08:53.900 --> 00:08:57.370
- What have you proven
this year so far?

00:08:58.638 --> 00:09:00.406
- Um, in my mind, nothing yet.

00:09:00.406 --> 00:09:01.507
You know? Nothing yet.

00:09:01.507 --> 00:09:05.545
You know, but I've proven to myself that I
can be happy again

00:09:05.545 --> 00:09:08.548
playing basketball, you know?

00:09:08.648 --> 00:09:10.349
And just be free again playing.

00:09:10.349 --> 00:09:13.386
You know, I think more
so than anything,

00:09:13.386 --> 00:09:15.821
proving that first. And in the midst of
me doing that,

00:09:17.056 --> 00:09:19.759
going through the process
of this whole thing.

00:09:19.759 --> 00:09:23.963
You know, you building towards
something that we just going have to wait

00:09:23.963 --> 00:09:26.632
and see what it pan out
to be at the end of the year.

00:09:27.199 --> 00:09:30.036
- When did you realize, "OK,
I am happy again playing basketball."

00:09:32.438 --> 00:09:36.642
- Maybe this was my first time
making a decision in my professional life

00:09:37.710 --> 00:09:41.480
and my professional career what team I wanna
go to. You know,

00:09:41.681 --> 00:09:44.550
I got traded. You know,
really my first time

00:09:44.550 --> 00:09:47.787
dealing with free agency
and picking where I wanted to go.

00:09:47.853 --> 00:09:52.725
You know, that was kind of a relief,
you know, going into my 13th year saying,

00:09:53.125 --> 00:09:55.895
"OK, this is my first time
being able to make a choice."

00:09:55.895 --> 00:09:58.297
- Yeah.
- You know, and I made that choice.

00:09:58.297 --> 00:10:01.200
And with that, it was kind of
like a burden off my shoulders.

00:10:01.434 --> 00:10:03.536
Me just knowing
what I had to do next.

00:10:05.071 --> 00:10:06.439
So from there, you know,

00:10:06.439 --> 00:10:10.209
I just was at peace with my decision
and just rode it out from there.
- Yeah.

00:10:10.476 --> 00:10:12.311
So you said you're,
you've proven to yourself

00:10:12.311 --> 00:10:16.282
you can be happy again playing basketball.
When you were unhappy playing basketball,

00:10:16.315 --> 00:10:17.583
what does that look like for you?

00:10:17.583 --> 00:10:20.319
You don't enjoy the moments.

00:10:20.720 --> 00:10:23.756
You don't enjoy the ups
and downs. You know,

00:10:25.124 --> 00:10:27.693
you don't enjoy what comes your way.

00:10:27.693 --> 00:10:31.030
You don't enjoy, you know,

00:10:31.030 --> 00:10:35.234
everything that the game has to bring,
you know, the good, the bad and then the ugly.

00:10:35.401 --> 00:10:37.770
You know, you're
not always going to have great days.

00:10:38.404 --> 00:10:42.074
So when the great days come,
when the great days aren't there,

00:10:42.074 --> 00:10:45.444
you indulge in the negative side of things easier.
- Yeah.
- You know what I mean?

00:10:45.444 --> 00:10:48.981
And it kind of makes your days
go by a lot slower,

00:10:49.215 --> 00:10:51.083
your week go by a lot slower.

00:10:51.083 --> 00:10:54.720
You know, you carry
a lot of stuff with you on your shoulders

00:10:54.720 --> 00:10:58.491
when you go out there to perform,
you know, and it just, it

00:10:58.524 --> 00:11:00.493
don't feel—
- You feel it all.
- Yeah, you feel it.

00:11:00.493 --> 00:11:01.994
You know, you feel it. And I think

00:11:04.296 --> 00:11:06.432
that's what being free

00:11:06.432 --> 00:11:09.835
feels like now is just
- Yeah.
- enjoying every moment,

00:11:09.835 --> 00:11:13.873
even even the long nights, you know, you
enjoy it, because it comes with the job.

00:11:13.939 --> 00:11:15.708
You know what I mean? Being banged up,

00:11:15.708 --> 00:11:18.678
you enjoy it because it comes
with the competition.

00:11:18.678 --> 00:11:20.780
So
- Yeah.
- Just that

00:11:20.780 --> 00:11:24.283
part of it for me is, is,
was the transition for me.

00:11:24.850 --> 00:11:28.354
It's so funny and interesting
because with a lot of guys,

00:11:28.354 --> 00:11:31.724
they talk about how basketball goes from
being this thing

00:11:31.724 --> 00:11:34.794
you're good at and that you love to really being—
- Yeah.
- a job.

00:11:35.061 --> 00:11:36.696
- Yeah.
- And when you're first thinking about it,

00:11:36.696 --> 00:11:39.465
like, there's no way I'm going to think
playing basketball in the NBA

00:11:39.465 --> 00:11:44.070
is like a job, but you very quickly—
- Yeah.
- Like, ‘Oh, no, this is, this is work.’

00:11:44.070 --> 00:11:46.872
And it's going to be
some days that I don’t love this.

00:11:46.906 --> 00:11:49.842
Can you explain how you're still doing
something you're very good at,

00:11:50.309 --> 00:11:53.446
but there is, like, this other part of it
that weighs you down in some ways?

00:11:53.479 --> 00:11:56.816
Yeah, because you know,
you get engulfed in,

00:11:57.349 --> 00:12:01.120
you know, the celebrity of being
a professional athlete at times, too, to

00:12:01.120 --> 00:12:04.523
where, you know, for me personally, I love,

00:12:05.424 --> 00:12:07.560
I love being normal as much as I can.

00:12:07.693 --> 00:12:10.262
You know, I love just being normal.

00:12:10.329 --> 00:12:13.332
Like whatever that is,
I love being normal.

00:12:13.332 --> 00:12:17.103
If it's me walking outside to go

00:12:17.103 --> 00:12:21.474
to Starbucks, to get a tea and walk back
like a normal person, you know what I mean, like you,

00:12:21.474 --> 00:12:26.112
you love those moments,
but sometimes you realize you can't do it.

00:12:26.412 --> 00:12:29.982
And that could kind of,
you know, be a burden on you

00:12:29.982 --> 00:12:33.252
sometimes. Like, you know, I'm
just like you, I'm trying to get some tea.

00:12:33.285 --> 00:12:35.221
Can I enjoy the tea
like you’re enjoying the tea?

00:12:36.222 --> 00:12:39.024
And sometimes you
have those long nights where, you know,

00:12:39.759 --> 00:12:42.728
you got to take care of yourself
physically, you know, after a long

00:12:43.095 --> 00:12:45.831
week of playing and
you finally get a day off

00:12:45.831 --> 00:12:48.100
and you know, you just can't go hang out

00:12:48.134 --> 00:12:51.270
with your friends and do certain things
that your friends doing. You got to,

00:12:51.337 --> 00:12:54.540
you've got to kind of lock
in. You've got to understand what's next.

00:12:54.573 --> 00:12:58.444
You know, it's a consistent thing
that don't stop once you're in a season,

00:12:58.444 --> 00:13:00.946
you know. You’re playing every other day
or back-to-back,

00:13:00.946 --> 00:13:04.216
or you’re traveling,
or you, you’re in practice, film,

00:13:04.216 --> 00:13:06.051
you're taking care of yourself physically.

00:13:06.051 --> 00:13:07.720
Then you got to worry
about yourself mentally.

00:13:07.720 --> 00:13:10.256
You know, there's so many dynamics
that comes to it,

00:13:10.256 --> 00:13:12.992
especially if you want to be good
and successful at it.

00:13:13.025 --> 00:13:15.761
You know—
- Yeah.
- it's just not about. ‘I make a lot of money,

00:13:15.761 --> 00:13:16.796
I can just go have fun.’

00:13:16.796 --> 00:13:19.131
You know, when you’re a pro
and a professional at this,

00:13:19.465 --> 00:13:22.401
it’s a lot that comes with it,
 you know, and it's tough at times.

00:13:22.768 --> 00:13:25.838
So when it comes to being normal,
what do you do for you?

00:13:26.138 --> 00:13:29.708
Like, what's the DeMar
thing that makes you feel

00:13:30.676 --> 00:13:34.146
normal?
- I mean, watch cartoons, like—
- What cartoon?

00:13:34.180 --> 00:13:36.215
I mean, I'm always a Simpsons fan.

00:13:36.215 --> 00:13:38.884
I grew up watching
The Simpsons, you know.

00:13:41.287 --> 00:13:42.721
Just kid things, you know?

00:13:42.721 --> 00:13:47.560
I find myself trying to tap in to
my childhood at times, you know, and just,

00:13:48.060 --> 00:13:52.331
you know, try to relive those moments,
whatever they may be.

00:13:52.331 --> 00:13:54.767
Sometimes I may get
a coloring book and color

00:13:55.501 --> 00:13:59.505
a color, like, a character, you know—
- Yeah.
- just to see if I still got it.

00:13:59.572 --> 00:14:02.741
You know, and trying
to find some peace in that, you know.

00:14:03.142 --> 00:14:07.079
So it’s just, it's
 just, it changes throughout the moments.

00:14:07.112 --> 00:14:08.047
But for me, it's just like

00:14:08.047 --> 00:14:12.585
you just try to tap in
and just be a kid as much as you can.

00:14:12.852 --> 00:14:14.119
Do you stay in the lines?

00:14:14.119 --> 00:14:15.387
Yeah, you got to stay in the lines.

00:14:15.387 --> 00:14:17.623
You know, you try to—
- No. 1 rule.
- You got to.

00:14:18.424 --> 00:14:23.395
Well, hey, luckily for you, you are surrounded
by lots of young people on the Bulls.

00:14:24.129 --> 00:14:27.099
How does it feel to be the vet?
- Man, it's crazy cuz

00:14:28.133 --> 00:14:30.102
I don’t think I never—
I haven't talked about this yet.

00:14:30.102 --> 00:14:35.241
But it's crazy because like whenever music—
like, we listen to music at practice,

00:14:35.241 --> 00:14:39.144
and even when we’re in our layup lines in a game,
it'd be certain songs that come on.

00:14:39.144 --> 00:14:41.313
And I always ask the guys like,
‘You know who this is?’

00:14:42.114 --> 00:14:45.351
They’d be like, "I never heard this song
in my life," and it could be like a Mary J.

00:14:45.351 --> 00:14:46.752
Blige song or something, you know what I mean?
- Yeah.

00:14:46.752 --> 00:14:50.823
It could be like, it could be a song
from 2005, and they have no clue.

00:14:50.856 --> 00:14:52.424
- They’re like, I was just born.
- Yeah.

00:14:52.424 --> 00:14:57.162
And it's like, "Well, I was born
in 2001," and it’s like, "Oh, my God." Like,

00:14:57.529 --> 00:15:01.500
but at the same time,
you know, it's enjoyable

00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:05.537
cuz you learn so much from 'em,
and they teach you so much

00:15:05.537 --> 00:15:09.008
in the same token. And once you, like,

00:15:10.109 --> 00:15:11.343
accept that, it's fun.

00:15:11.343 --> 00:15:15.147
You know, I think one of the guys
one day in practice was like,

00:15:15.147 --> 00:15:16.115
"Man, you the only person on the team
born in the '80s.

00:15:16.115 --> 00:15:20.085
What it feel like?"
And I'm like, "I'm born in '89.

00:15:20.085 --> 00:15:24.290
I'm only 32." Like, you know what I mean?
- [Laughing]

00:15:24.323 --> 00:15:28.227
- But it's, it's crazy, you know, to have that back and forth
and we have fun with it.

00:15:28.227 --> 00:15:31.130
So it's definitely cool
just to, just to have that

00:15:31.130 --> 00:15:33.399
because I learn so much
from those guys as well.

00:15:33.432 --> 00:15:36.435
- Yeah. Born in the '80s. It's '89.
- I'm born in—I'm like, yeah.

00:15:37.336 --> 00:15:39.838
It's crazy.
- And what music they trying to put you on?

00:15:39.838 --> 00:15:43.075
Like NBA YoungBoy or something?
- Everything. You name it, it be some people.

00:15:43.208 --> 00:15:48.314
But, but even that, I listen to it.
Like man, who hot right now?

00:15:48.314 --> 00:15:50.716
I listen to it just so I can be on the same page
as them as well, you know what I mean?

00:15:51.550 --> 00:15:53.152
- Yeah.
- So it be cool.

00:15:53.152 --> 00:15:56.922
- So when did you realize you were the vet?
Was it when they were like '89?

00:15:57.690 --> 00:15:59.591
- Yeah, I think it was like training camp.

00:15:59.591 --> 00:16:02.995
It was, it was somewhere around there.
I think we was listening to like,

00:16:03.929 --> 00:16:05.597
50 Cent "Get Rich or Die Tryin'" album.

00:16:06.665 --> 00:16:08.300
And somebody was like, "Yeah,
I was like one years old, two years old when"—

00:16:08.300 --> 00:16:12.371
and I was like, "I remember buying that CD."
You know what I mean?

00:16:13.005 --> 00:16:15.841
- That was all anybody cared about. Yeah.
- Yeah, yeah. Like I remember, I told them,

00:16:15.941 --> 00:16:20.045
I was like, "Yo, I remember going to the store,
buyin' that. And you just was—"

00:16:20.245 --> 00:16:23.649
And that was the moment.
That was my first moment.

00:16:23.682 --> 00:16:25.918
I was like, ah yeah,
it's gonna be a long—

00:16:26.018 --> 00:16:27.152
it's gonna be a long year.

00:16:27.152 --> 00:16:29.355
- Oh yeah, you gotta get them
on all that stuff.

00:16:29.421 --> 00:16:33.025
They probably haven't heard
even like, old Wayne—yeah, you have to.

00:16:34.093 --> 00:16:37.930
- It's so much—I asked them about Jay-Z.
They was like, "Yeah, I heard about two, three Jay-Z songs."

00:16:39.331 --> 00:16:40.699
I'm like…I feel offended.
- For sure.

00:16:40.699 --> 00:16:43.268
- You know what I mean? Like, I feel offended!
- Yeah!

00:16:43.402 --> 00:16:46.271
Oh no. My gosh, no.
Then you have some teaching to do.

00:16:46.338 --> 00:16:50.809
Teaching some real life lessons.
- No, I do. I do. But that's, in their credit,

00:16:51.610 --> 00:16:53.412
they, they listen.
- Yeah.

00:16:53.412 --> 00:16:55.681
- You know, whatever it is,
not even just music,

00:16:55.681 --> 00:16:59.151
just anything in general, even with movies.
Like, I do the same thing with movies.

00:16:59.151 --> 00:17:03.255
Have you seen this movie, this movie?
And a lot of them, like it was one guy—

00:17:03.589 --> 00:17:05.557
I'mma say his name, Patrick Williams—on the team.

00:17:06.625 --> 00:17:08.660
I asked him, I made a reference,

00:17:08.660 --> 00:17:12.731
one game we was playing,
I made a reference from "300," the movie "300."

00:17:13.132 --> 00:17:14.867
And he didn't get it.
I was like, "You know what '300' is?"

00:17:14.867 --> 00:17:18.737
He said, "No."
I said, "It's a movie." He said, "What?"

00:17:19.271 --> 00:17:21.740
He said, "Man, I'm gonna go home
and watch it tonight." So he went home,

00:17:21.740 --> 00:17:25.110
watched it. Called me after the movie, said,
"Oh my God, that movie was amazing.

00:17:25.310 --> 00:17:28.180
You got another movie for me?"
You know what I mean?

00:17:28.213 --> 00:17:30.949
- Oh, that's great.
What'd you say, like, "This is Sparta" or something?

00:17:31.517 --> 00:17:32.584
- Yeah. It was crazy. It was crazy.
- [Laughing]

00:17:32.584 --> 00:17:36.021
- But he went to go watch it and he,
you know?

00:17:36.055 --> 00:17:38.057
- That's great. I love that they're,
they're coachable.

00:17:38.057 --> 00:17:39.425
- Definitely coachable. Definitely coachable.
- Yeah, they're totally coachable.

00:17:40.025 --> 00:17:42.428
So tell me, what makes a good vet?

00:17:43.262 --> 00:17:44.997
- Um, something like that.

00:17:44.997 --> 00:17:46.165
Just listen.
- Yeah.

00:17:47.232 --> 00:17:49.635
- You know, um, you know,
you gotta,

00:17:49.635 --> 00:17:51.103
you let guys be guys.

00:17:51.103 --> 00:17:54.873
But you teach 'em if they make
any mistakes, you know, show 'em why

00:17:55.541 --> 00:17:56.809
or why they shouldn't do something,

00:17:56.809 --> 00:18:01.246
you know, and along the way
and kind of be relatable to 'em.

00:18:01.480 --> 00:18:04.283
You know what I mean? Every vet
I always had was always relatable,

00:18:04.316 --> 00:18:07.286
always talked to me.
They didn't make anything feel forced.

00:18:07.319 --> 00:18:09.688
You know, they was, they cared about me
first and foremost,

00:18:10.222 --> 00:18:13.692
not just as a player,
just as a person in general,

00:18:13.692 --> 00:18:17.196
you know, and I learned so much
from all the vets that I had

00:18:17.396 --> 00:18:19.932
my first couple of years in the league,
and I kind of carry that over

00:18:20.232 --> 00:18:23.335
to just teaching these guys, you know
what I mean? You really genuinely

00:18:23.335 --> 00:18:25.104
wanting the best for them on and off the court.

00:18:25.104 --> 00:18:27.172
And I think whatever comes with that
makes a good vet.

00:18:27.339 --> 00:18:30.042
- Yeah. Who's been your best vet?

00:18:30.442 --> 00:18:32.411
- My best vet?
- Yeah.

00:18:34.179 --> 00:18:38.117
- Jarrett Jack was—he was,
he was my favorite vet, you know, to this day.

00:18:38.250 --> 00:18:43.956
I talk to J-Jack, you know,
he call me randomly just to talk mess.

00:18:43.989 --> 00:18:46.758
But he was, he was one guy
that was definitely instrumental

00:18:47.059 --> 00:18:48.560
in my career early on.

00:18:48.560 --> 00:18:50.829
- OK, I love this.
On that team, obviously,

00:18:50.863 --> 00:18:54.533
you have Alex Caruso, right now
is unfortunately sidelined.

00:18:54.900 --> 00:18:57.736
Lonzo Ball sidelined.
When you know, you know,

00:18:57.769 --> 00:19:00.606
two guys are going down, two key guys.
- Yeah.

00:19:01.140 --> 00:19:01.840
- Where does your mind go?

00:19:01.840 --> 00:19:05.210
What do you say to the team
to make sure you guys are still locked in?

00:19:05.511 --> 00:19:06.712
- Um, just keep the confidence high.

00:19:06.712 --> 00:19:07.479
Understand it's an opportunity

00:19:07.479 --> 00:19:12.651
that someone else may not had

00:19:12.651 --> 00:19:15.721
if these guys were healthy
to be able to have a chance to,

00:19:15.721 --> 00:19:19.691
you know, fill in that role, you know,
and that's what a team is made of,

00:19:19.691 --> 00:19:23.862
guys understanding when, when one key guy
go down, what can you do to benefit

00:19:23.862 --> 00:19:27.266
and help and fill that void to continue
to keep us successful?

00:19:27.332 --> 00:19:29.568
And it's a chance to learn and to grow,

00:19:29.868 --> 00:19:33.705
you know, and approach that position
with nothing but confidence.

00:19:33.739 --> 00:19:38.010
So for me, that's all I do,
and I tell guys, just be confident,

00:19:38.076 --> 00:19:40.145
you know what I mean? Don't overthink it,
don't feel pressured.

00:19:40.179 --> 00:19:43.215
You know, we all in this together.
So that's one thing I definitely share.

00:19:43.849 --> 00:19:47.586
- I just really love this
like evolution of your career

00:19:47.619 --> 00:19:50.656
that has happened
and the path that it has gone on, because

00:19:50.689 --> 00:19:52.858
would you say it's accurate to say
this has been like, I mean, based on the normal,

00:19:53.559 --> 00:19:57.496
like a late-career surge? Would you call it that?
- Yeah, yeah, for sure.

00:19:57.996 --> 00:20:03.936
- So, is this how you envisioned
 your NBA career going?

00:20:04.203 --> 00:20:06.371
- For me, I always envision anything I do,

00:20:06.371 --> 00:20:09.341
I want to continue to get better at it.
You know, whatever

00:20:09.975 --> 00:20:11.143
that look like, I want to

00:20:11.143 --> 00:20:14.046
continue to get better at it,
and whatever opportunity I can have

00:20:14.213 --> 00:20:17.049
in getting better at it, I'm gonna
 try to fill that

00:20:17.049 --> 00:20:19.885
to my highest potential.
- Yeah.

00:20:20.352 --> 00:20:22.521
- You know? And that's the challenge
 I take on every single year.

00:20:23.689 --> 00:20:26.858
I never look at, you know, years
 as being a down year.

00:20:26.858 --> 00:20:30.796
You kind of look at years as a growing
 year. You know, you planted whatever seed.

00:20:30.796 --> 00:20:33.966
Sometimes it takes time

00:20:33.966 --> 00:20:37.336
for you to grow and fully understand
 what was planted, you know?

00:20:37.336 --> 00:20:38.170
And I think as long

00:20:38.170 --> 00:20:41.940
as you keep planting seeds and
 allowing them to grow and be patient,

00:20:41.940 --> 00:20:44.142
you know, you're going to continue
 to stay consistent

00:20:44.142 --> 00:20:47.879
in whatever you're doing. If that's gardening,
 if it's farming, whatever it is,

00:20:47.879 --> 00:20:51.850
you know, as long as you continue to do it
 and you continue to see your process.

00:20:52.517 --> 00:20:55.120
- OK, that final year with San Antonio,

00:20:55.954 --> 00:20:58.290
what seed did you plant?

00:20:59.891 --> 00:21:03.629
- Um...Damn, I wish I knew some...I planted
 probably a cornfield.

00:21:04.596 --> 00:21:05.931
You know?
- Why a cornfield?

00:21:05.931 --> 00:21:08.300
- I don't know, because you just sound
 like a lot will grow

00:21:08.300 --> 00:21:09.534
and you could feed a lot of people with it.
- Yeah.

00:21:09.534 --> 00:21:12.771
- No, it was...It was one of those seeds of,

00:21:13.872 --> 00:21:14.406
you know, I've got to

00:21:14.406 --> 00:21:17.442
give credit to all the guys
 I played with in San Antonio.

00:21:17.442 --> 00:21:21.313
Even a lot of young guys that, you know,
 they motivated me and pushed me, um,

00:21:22.681 --> 00:21:24.716
in a sense of having so much respect

00:21:24.716 --> 00:21:27.586
for me and my game and allowing me to be them.

00:21:27.819 --> 00:21:30.889
And helping them
 to the best of their abilities.

00:21:32.190 --> 00:21:35.560
And it created like
 a bond of me understanding

00:21:36.328 --> 00:21:39.765
basketball from a full perspective
 and not just yourself.

00:21:39.898 --> 00:21:43.902
You know, I stopped
 caring about myself, so to speak.

00:21:43.902 --> 00:21:47.639
You know, it was moreso a team aspect
 when I looked at all

00:21:47.639 --> 00:21:52.577
the guys I played with in San Antonio,
 you know, and it opened,

00:21:52.577 --> 00:21:55.013
it made me more open-minded

00:21:55.013 --> 00:22:00.419
of having a higher IQ, approaching
the game from a whole perspective

00:22:00.419 --> 00:22:02.187
and not feeling like, you know,

00:22:02.187 --> 00:22:05.090
I'm Superman. I need to go out there
 and do it all by yourself.

00:22:05.090 --> 00:22:08.627
So, that definitely was a career lesson
 for me that I learned from

00:22:09.261 --> 00:22:11.997
every single player.
Even playing for Pop.

00:22:12.130 --> 00:22:15.634
You know, that was one thing that
 he helped me unlock mentally from my game.

00:22:16.401 --> 00:22:19.004
- I would say that,
 you know, most players in the league,

00:22:19.137 --> 00:22:22.007
they just want respect from their peers.

00:22:22.240 --> 00:22:23.141
- Most definitely.

00:22:23.141 --> 00:22:26.378
- When do you feel like you got that respect

00:22:26.378 --> 00:22:29.514
and that kind of vindication
 within the league?

00:22:29.881 --> 00:22:31.483
- Um...that's crazy

00:22:31.483 --> 00:22:33.618
you say that, because that's all I ever...

00:22:33.618 --> 00:22:35.921
that's all I ever wanted. That was...

00:22:35.921 --> 00:22:38.924
It wasn't about how much money you made.

00:22:39.891 --> 00:22:42.160
None of that. Like,
 I didn't want to go outside.

00:22:42.160 --> 00:22:44.062
I didn't want to go party.
 I didn't want to hang out.

00:22:44.062 --> 00:22:45.330
I didn't want to go on vacations 'til

00:22:45.330 --> 00:22:47.432
I felt accepted by my peers. And I think

00:22:48.967 --> 00:22:50.802
probably my fourth year in league,

00:22:50.802 --> 00:22:54.139
fourth, fifth year in the league,
 think I made my first All-Star Game, too.

00:22:54.139 --> 00:22:58.643
And you know, you just felt like you
 belonged, and you was accepted by your peers.

00:22:58.643 --> 00:23:00.011
Your peers respected you

00:23:00.011 --> 00:23:02.814
when you stepped out there on the court,
 you know. And that's the highest

00:23:03.415 --> 00:23:07.619
compliment you could have in
 this league is when your peers respect

00:23:07.619 --> 00:23:11.590
you, your game inside and out,
 and you as a, you as a person.

00:23:11.590 --> 00:23:13.992
You know, that goes a long way
to adding respect in this league.

00:23:13.992 --> 00:23:16.361
So, I'd say my fourth, fifth year.

00:23:16.361 --> 00:23:19.998
- So do you still continue
to seek that validation,

00:23:19.998 --> 00:23:22.501
or do you think you're comfortable
with the things that you've accomplished?

00:23:22.534 --> 00:23:24.403
- Nah, I'm not comfortable at all.

00:23:24.403 --> 00:23:27.939
I don't think I will ever be comfortable
until, you know,

00:23:28.273 --> 00:23:32.978
I decide to walk away from it, you know.
And I think that's the beauty of playing

00:23:32.978 --> 00:23:37.382
this game is to give everything
 for as long as you can to it

00:23:37.416 --> 00:23:40.819
so by the time you walk away, you
 you could walk away feeling

00:23:41.319 --> 00:23:45.157
at ease with walking away, you know?
 And that's one thing I want to,

00:23:45.690 --> 00:23:48.493
you know, knock on wood,
 have the opportunity to be able to do

00:23:48.760 --> 00:23:51.897
is to walk away from the game
 at peace, you know.

00:23:51.897 --> 00:23:55.300
And that was one thing, you know,
 Coach always used to talk to me about,

00:23:56.501 --> 00:23:58.904
you know, that's when you know.
When you know

00:23:58.904 --> 00:24:03.208
you can walk away at ease from the game,
when you have peace.

00:24:03.208 --> 00:24:06.111
- Yeah. And what do you think
 that peace looks like?

00:24:06.411 --> 00:24:08.280
- I dunno. You just keep striving.

00:24:08.280 --> 00:24:12.217
You know, you just give
 everything you got into this game.

00:24:12.250 --> 00:24:14.753
until you feel like
 you can't give no more.

00:24:14.886 --> 00:24:19.090
Then you look back at everything you did
 and you may realize

00:24:19.090 --> 00:24:20.659
like, "Damn, I did a lot of things."

00:24:20.659 --> 00:24:24.963
- Yeah.
- You know, and evaluate it,

00:24:25.730 --> 00:24:27.999
it's what you comfortable with at that point.
- Mm-hmm.

00:24:28.567 --> 00:24:31.136
Sometimes when guys talk about,
 you know, when they realize

00:24:31.136 --> 00:24:32.204
they have the respect of their peers

00:24:32.204 --> 00:24:35.974
and they felt vindicated,
 they can remember like a specific guy

00:24:36.308 --> 00:24:40.045
saying something to them, and they said,
 "Oh, when he knew I was nice."

00:24:40.212 --> 00:24:41.513
- Wow.
- "I knew I was nice."

00:24:41.513 --> 00:24:45.150
- That's a...
- Is someone like that for you?

00:24:45.851 --> 00:24:47.819
- Yeah. And it's crazy. It's crazy. That's...

00:24:49.221 --> 00:24:50.956
One person who

00:24:50.956 --> 00:24:54.059
my rookie year told me something
 that stuck with me.

00:24:54.826 --> 00:24:57.762
I don't even know if he remember
 this, is Michael Redd.

00:24:58.763 --> 00:25:03.268
He told me I was gonna be special
in this league. Keep, keep,

00:25:03.802 --> 00:25:07.939
don't stop. Like,
I just remember those words so vividly.

00:25:08.306 --> 00:25:12.310
"You gonna be special in this league.
Don't stop. Keep working." It was my rookie year,

00:25:12.744 --> 00:25:15.247
Michael Redd. I never got
the chance to tell him

00:25:15.380 --> 00:25:16.147
thank you for that.

00:25:16.147 --> 00:25:18.550
He might, he may not even remember.
- Yeah.

00:25:18.550 --> 00:25:23.054
- But Michael Redd told me those words,
and it stuck with me to this day.
- Really?

00:25:23.154 --> 00:25:26.992
Why do you think in that moment, like, that
was so, it resonated so deeply for you?

00:25:26.992 --> 00:25:30.929
- I don't know. At that time, you know,
he was a veteran.

00:25:31.129 --> 00:25:33.298
You know, he was an established player.

00:25:33.298 --> 00:25:37.035
He was a player, like, I've always been
a fan of the game of basketball.

00:25:37.903 --> 00:25:39.604
'70s, '80s, '90s.

00:25:39.604 --> 00:25:43.542
So I always respected
all the older guys.
- Mmhmm.
- Honest.

00:25:43.542 --> 00:25:46.411
I respected what Michael Redd did
in the league.

00:25:48.113 --> 00:25:48.713
And I don't know.

00:25:48.713 --> 00:25:50.081
It's just that moment where he pulled me

00:25:50.081 --> 00:25:54.452
to the side and, you know,
I felt how genuine he was about it,

00:25:54.452 --> 00:25:57.889
you know what I mean? Because even at that point,
my rookie year, you know,

00:25:59.057 --> 00:26:01.893
you deal with so many emotions

00:26:02.127 --> 00:26:05.630
questioning, like, damn, "When am I gonna be good?
When Imma be able to be myself?

00:26:05.630 --> 00:26:09.200
When am I gonna be comfortable?" Like, I'm
only 19 years old playing with grown men.

00:26:09.601 --> 00:26:13.138
So for him to pull me to the side
and acknowledge something

00:26:13.238 --> 00:26:17.309
towards me that I was feeling
iffy about was all I needed.

00:26:17.709 --> 00:26:20.178
- Shoutout Michael Redd.
- Shoutout Michael Redd.
- I love that.

00:26:20.545 --> 00:26:23.415
OK.
-  I never told nobody that either.
I never told nobody.

00:26:24.182 --> 00:26:27.018
- Look at us!
- It's crazy.
- We do this.
- Yeah, you got it.
- You know, we get the stories.

00:26:27.819 --> 00:26:30.021
So, all right, looking at your career.

00:26:30.021 --> 00:26:32.023
Haven't been to the NBA Finals.
- Mmhmm.

00:26:32.023 --> 00:26:35.460
- How often do you think about that?
- All the time. All the time. You know,

00:26:36.661 --> 00:26:39.331
the older you get, the more years that pass,

00:26:39.464 --> 00:26:43.868
you watch Finals after Finals,
and, you know, you come into your first game

00:26:43.868 --> 00:26:47.372
of the season, you strive
to want to make the Finals.

00:26:47.672 --> 00:26:50.041
You know, you want to be
the last team playing.

00:26:50.375 --> 00:26:52.344
You know, that's your motivation.

00:26:52.344 --> 00:26:56.114
If you're a true competitor in this game,
you wanna go to the Finals.

00:26:56.114 --> 00:26:57.782
You wanna know what that feel like.

00:26:57.782 --> 00:27:00.018
You want to have a chance to compete for

00:27:00.986 --> 00:27:03.054
the trophy, you know, so

00:27:03.054 --> 00:27:07.192
it always played on my mind,
you know, and I always watch it.

00:27:07.258 --> 00:27:12.197
You know, you always find inspiration,
motivation and just want to get there

00:27:12.197 --> 00:27:15.767
and see what happens, you know.
So it's something I always think about.

00:27:16.501 --> 00:27:19.204
- So when you're thinking about the NBA

00:27:19.204 --> 00:27:22.374
Finals or not going, what's
running through your mind?

00:27:22.374 --> 00:27:25.076
Is it ever a moment of, like, OK, like,
I need to get there;

00:27:25.076 --> 00:27:27.746
the time is now?
What's the emotion?

00:27:27.812 --> 00:27:28.980
- I mean, just stay the course.

00:27:28.980 --> 00:27:32.684
You know, I think, I
look at things day by day.

00:27:32.684 --> 00:27:35.987
You know, you work towards
something day by day.

00:27:36.154 --> 00:27:37.122
You can't look at it,

00:27:37.122 --> 00:27:40.358
you know, a week from now,
a month from now. It's like, all right,

00:27:40.358 --> 00:27:44.729
what can I do today to be my best today
and do the same tomorrow and tomorrow?

00:27:44.963 --> 00:27:48.033
And hopefully, by the time
you get to that final day,

00:27:48.466 --> 00:27:52.270
you look up and you're in the Finals. You
know, and that's how I approach it

00:27:52.370 --> 00:27:53.972
because you don't want to
drive yourself too crazy.
- Yeah.

00:27:53.972 --> 00:27:56.141
- Like, I need to make the Finals.
 I need to make the Finals.

00:27:56.508 --> 00:27:58.576
And sometimes they don't
necessarily go that way.

00:27:58.610 --> 00:28:00.345
So for me, it's just, you know,

00:28:01.479 --> 00:28:02.514
taking it day by day.

00:28:02.514 --> 00:28:06.017
- Yeah. And I really do
 think sometimes people underestimate

00:28:06.017 --> 00:28:09.154
just how difficult that road
to the Finals is.

00:28:09.220 --> 00:28:13.058
- Yeah.
- Like, winning a championship is no tiny feat.

00:28:13.224 --> 00:28:16.127
And winning games is, like, only some of it.
- Yeah.

00:28:16.194 --> 00:28:20.331
- You know, there's so much other work
- So much, yeah.
- that really goes into that.

00:28:20.632 --> 00:28:22.867
What are your thoughts on ring culture?

00:28:22.901 --> 00:28:26.037
Do you think that we put
too much emphasis on a player's,

00:28:26.037 --> 00:28:30.241
like, worth based on their championships
or their rings?
- I think so.

00:28:30.542 --> 00:28:33.178
You know, I'd always
go back and look at,

00:28:33.445 --> 00:28:34.746
you know, you can look at all the great,

00:28:34.746 --> 00:28:36.915
there's so many great players
that don't have rings.

00:28:37.315 --> 00:28:40.919
And if you bring up that player versus
another player who do have a ring,

00:28:41.419 --> 00:28:45.790
you will discredit that great player by saying he don't have a ring.
- Right.
- But that don't,

00:28:46.825 --> 00:28:48.293
you can't take away from all

00:28:48.293 --> 00:28:51.362
the accomplishments he had in his career
because some people don't even

00:28:52.530 --> 00:28:54.666
get half of what some of these

00:28:54.666 --> 00:28:57.902
great players have to
because they don't have a ring.

00:28:57.902 --> 00:29:01.773
You want to, you know, say he not like
 this player because he have a ring.

00:29:01.806 --> 00:29:05.577
So,
- Mmhmm.
- you know, at times I do feel like,
 you know, it's a lot thrown on it.

00:29:05.710 --> 00:29:09.447
You know, because you ask
 all those competitors, they wish

00:29:09.447 --> 00:29:12.484
they had a ring.
- Yeah.
- You know, that's the ultimate thing for us all to have.
- Mmhmm.

00:29:12.484 --> 00:29:16.554
- But you know, everybody can't have a ring.

00:29:17.055 --> 00:29:21.493
But that don't mean you can't work
your butt off and be the best teammate,

00:29:21.493 --> 00:29:25.063
best player you can be throughout
your whole career.
- Mmhmm.

00:29:25.430 --> 00:29:29.634
That was one of the things I loved about,
you know, the Finals last season.

00:29:29.634 --> 00:29:33.037
It's, like, I wanted to see someone
 get their first ring.
- For sure.

00:29:33.071 --> 00:29:35.540
- Like, I either wanted to see Chris
 Paul and Devin Booker get it.

00:29:35.740 --> 00:29:38.810
Wanted to see Giannis get it. It was exciting
cuz it was someone was going

00:29:38.810 --> 00:29:42.180
to finally accomplish this thing
that it's all we talk about.
- Yep.

00:29:42.213 --> 00:29:44.916
- So it was really cool to watch that.

00:29:44.949 --> 00:29:46.551
You certainly are a great player.

00:29:46.551 --> 00:29:48.887
I wanna throw this out to you,
which I'm sure you know.

00:29:49.020 --> 00:29:49.988
But, you know, against the Magic,

00:29:49.988 --> 00:29:53.792
You had a season-high 41 points,
but you did it

00:29:53.792 --> 00:29:56.361
without taking a single three-pointer.

00:29:56.995 --> 00:29:59.264
Are you, like, the mid-range king?

00:29:59.931 --> 00:30:04.169
- I mean, I…
- [Laughing]
- People crowned me that. They say it.
 I see it. It's an honor.

00:30:04.169 --> 00:30:05.103
I appreciate it.

00:30:05.103 --> 00:30:08.573
- Yeah.
- I just stay true to myself.

00:30:08.740 --> 00:30:12.210
You know, I try to master
whatever it is I do on that court

00:30:12.443 --> 00:30:15.113
to the best of my abilities
and go out there feel like,

00:30:15.413 --> 00:30:19.317
you know, I'm the best at whatever I do
when I step out there on that court.

00:30:19.450 --> 00:30:21.486
You know, you would never hear me say

00:30:22.587 --> 00:30:25.256
I'm the mid-range king and all this
cuz, you know,

00:30:25.623 --> 00:30:29.027
I learned so much from a lot of those—
I talk to CP all the time

00:30:29.027 --> 00:30:31.196
about how he get, you know,

00:30:31.196 --> 00:30:35.200
to the mid-range and, you know, how
he take advantage of so many things.

00:30:35.200 --> 00:30:37.168
You know, I watch all the great players.

00:30:37.168 --> 00:30:39.637
I watch Kawhi. I watch KD. I watch,

00:30:40.071 --> 00:30:40.839
I learned so much

00:30:40.839 --> 00:30:45.210
from all these players that helped me
be the player, you know, that I am today.

00:30:45.210 --> 00:30:50.148
So it's hard for me to just say I'm
the best or I'm the greatest at something,
you know what I mean?

00:30:50.181 --> 00:30:53.251
But…
- Yeah.
- I feel like when I go out there, you know,

00:30:53.251 --> 00:30:57.188
I work my butt off to be great at what I do.
- Yes, and—but

00:30:57.222 --> 00:30:59.657
you know you're very good at it?
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:30:59.657 --> 00:31:02.794
- OK. People do call that mid-range shot,
 like, a lost art.
- Yeah.

00:31:02.827 --> 00:31:04.662
- Why do you think they refer to it that way?

00:31:05.630 --> 00:31:08.466
- Because you don't see too many people

00:31:08.466 --> 00:31:10.869
doing it at that level.

00:31:10.869 --> 00:31:14.806
Myself may do it. CP may do
it. Devin Booker may do it.

00:31:15.039 --> 00:31:18.409
KD may do it. Kawhi may do it.
You know, it's a select

00:31:18.710 --> 00:31:22.881
few guys who dominate the mid-range,

00:31:22.981 --> 00:31:24.616
and in the same token, you

00:31:24.616 --> 00:31:26.885
look around the league,
you get so many other players

00:31:26.885 --> 00:31:29.787
that, you know, launch
threes so much, you know?

00:31:29.787 --> 00:31:33.057
So, you know, it's just a narrative that's painted out there

00:31:33.057 --> 00:31:36.261
because, you know, it's still a necessity of the game.

00:31:36.461 --> 00:31:40.932
The mid-range shot is still a necessity
and a need in the game.
- Mmhmm.

00:31:40.932 --> 00:31:45.503
- But you know, we emphasize three-point
shooting so much.

00:31:45.937 --> 00:31:47.972
And it's hard not to when you have, you know,

00:31:47.972 --> 00:31:50.942
Steph Curry, Klay Thompson. You have,
you know, Trae Young.

00:31:50.975 --> 00:31:54.012
You have so many players
that do that at a high level as well.

00:31:54.345 --> 00:31:57.181
But, you know, I think the guys that's
keeping it mid-range

00:31:57.181 --> 00:32:01.019
is definitely balancing
it back out.
- Yeah.

00:32:01.019 --> 00:32:04.789
But because it feels like there
is this major push for teams to

00:32:04.789 --> 00:32:07.659
just be so heavy on the three,
- Yeah.
 - was there a moment for you,

00:32:07.659 --> 00:32:10.061
you thought, do I need to adapt to this?

00:32:10.395 --> 00:32:12.830
- No. I just always, what—

00:32:12.830 --> 00:32:14.499
It's another thing Kobe told me.

00:32:14.499 --> 00:32:16.434
He just always told me,
stay true to myself.

00:32:16.434 --> 00:32:18.569
You know, I remember he tell me that way
back in

00:32:19.070 --> 00:32:22.941
2015. Just to stay true to yourself,
you know, stay the course.

00:32:23.341 --> 00:32:26.044
No matter what somebody may say,
you keep working at your craft,

00:32:26.344 --> 00:32:29.047
you become unstoppable at something.

00:32:29.047 --> 00:32:30.982
Until I figure that out,

00:32:30.982 --> 00:32:32.283
be true to yourself.

00:32:32.283 --> 00:32:37.055
And those words stuck with me to this day,
you know, so I never wanted to give in

00:32:37.055 --> 00:32:38.690
into just coming down, you know,

00:32:39.691 --> 00:32:43.561
shooting threes, threes, threes, threes
when I know I could get to a certain spot

00:32:43.962 --> 00:32:47.065
that I know is unguardable.

00:32:48.299 --> 00:32:51.302
Until that is stopped, Imma continue to do it.

00:32:51.302 --> 00:32:54.405
- Yeah. It's not broke,
 don't fix it.
- Yeah.
- Yeah. OK,

00:32:54.405 --> 00:32:55.907
a lot of season left,

00:32:55.907 --> 00:32:59.477
but are you the MVP?
- Me the MVP?

00:33:00.545 --> 00:33:02.647
- Mmhmm.
- Yeah, I came in this season thinking
I was the MVP.

00:33:02.814 --> 00:33:05.883
- OK.
- You know, and that's
 just my mentality, you know,

00:33:05.883 --> 00:33:09.420
not in a selfish manner,
but just knowing the work that I put in.

00:33:09.487 --> 00:33:12.390
And not just for myself as well. For,

00:33:12.890 --> 00:33:15.660
you know, my team. You know, my team

00:33:16.928 --> 00:33:20.264
has overcome so much so
quick, you know,

00:33:20.298 --> 00:33:23.568
and it sucks that we are dealing
with so many injuries of late, but

00:33:25.370 --> 00:33:27.605
that's—the team we had pushed me

00:33:28.539 --> 00:33:30.842
and allowed me to be the player I knew

00:33:30.842 --> 00:33:35.313
I was, you know, and that showed.
You know, my own mentality

00:33:35.646 --> 00:33:38.383
coming into this season
felt like I was the best player,

00:33:38.416 --> 00:33:41.219
you know, dealing with so much doubt and
so much criticism.

00:33:41.686 --> 00:33:43.755
For whatever reason, you know,

00:33:44.522 --> 00:33:48.026
I had that mentality coming in, saying,
Imma be the best me.

00:33:48.493 --> 00:33:53.631
And whatever comes with that, whether
if it's MVP, king of the mid-range,

00:33:53.731 --> 00:33:58.269
whatever. I was gonna take it.
- Yeah.

00:33:58.336 --> 00:34:01.439
What is the MVP to you? Cuz it, you know, it feels like
people have different criteria.
- Yeah.

00:34:01.506 --> 00:34:05.543
- For you, what is it?
- To me, the MVP is

00:34:06.611 --> 00:34:09.614
establishing a winning culture for one.

00:34:11.082 --> 00:34:13.418
For not just your teammates, for your organization.

00:34:15.053 --> 00:34:17.889
Being the ultimate leader.

00:34:18.322 --> 00:34:20.725
Dealing with adversity like it's

00:34:21.793 --> 00:34:24.162
a sunny day in the middle of Chicago.

00:34:25.530 --> 00:34:28.800
You know, just, it's so many responsibilities
 that come with that.

00:34:28.800 --> 00:34:32.503
And the ones that carry it on
on their shoulders like it's a feather.

00:34:33.204 --> 00:34:36.841
That's the MVP, you know,
night in and night out doing your job.

00:34:38.109 --> 00:34:39.911
Being banged up, being hurt,

00:34:39.911 --> 00:34:43.514
still taking on that challenge to push
and make your team successful.

00:34:43.981 --> 00:34:46.717
Being, you know, short-handed. Still

00:34:46.717 --> 00:34:49.887
coming to work, perform or doing whatever
you need to do to carry your team.

00:34:49.887 --> 00:34:54.625
To me, that's the MVP, you know,
and we have so many of them in our league

00:34:54.625 --> 00:34:58.029
that do it, you know. Someone
I love watching every single night.

00:34:58.062 --> 00:34:59.363
Chris Paul, you know. Chris Paul

00:34:59.363 --> 00:35:01.532
don't go out there and score
30, 40 points a night.

00:35:01.899 --> 00:35:05.603
But the way he carries
his team is the ultimate true MVP,

00:35:05.870 --> 00:35:09.040
you know, and he's been carrying himself
like an MVP every single year.

00:35:09.040 --> 00:35:12.110
Every team he go to
taking that winning culture, taking

00:35:12.110 --> 00:35:15.046
that leadership, taking that,
that next step in,

00:35:15.813 --> 00:35:18.449
you know, pushing his teammates
 and making everybody around him better.

00:35:18.749 --> 00:35:20.751
To me, that's an MVP. Yeah.

00:35:20.751 --> 00:35:23.121
- So what does MVP signify for you?

00:35:23.121 --> 00:35:25.423
Like, if you win MVP this season,

00:35:25.923 --> 00:35:27.825
what does it prove
to yourself as a player?

00:35:27.825 --> 00:35:29.460
Prove to others as a player?

00:35:29.460 --> 00:35:33.264
Um, it's never doubt,
never give up on yourself.

00:35:33.264 --> 00:35:36.567
Never feed into the negativity, the doubt.

00:35:38.669 --> 00:35:44.242
Um, when you truly believe in yourself
and you work towards something

00:35:44.242 --> 00:35:47.145
with everything in you, and you
believe in timing and patience.

00:35:48.079 --> 00:35:49.347
That's an MVP for me.

00:35:49.347 --> 00:35:51.849
You know, just being that inspiration of

00:35:52.283 --> 00:35:56.020
of, you know, you could be counted out
by the masses of people.

00:35:56.587 --> 00:35:59.423
But as long as you believe in yourself,
that's all that matters

00:35:59.423 --> 00:36:00.925
and you will forever be an MVP.

00:36:00.925 --> 00:36:05.263
Whether someone give you a trophy or not,
you know? So to me, it is something

00:36:05.530 --> 00:36:09.433
that you carry internally
because, you know, without your,

00:36:10.935 --> 00:36:15.339
your confidence in everything you believe
in, you let other people take away

00:36:15.339 --> 00:36:20.011
because of word of mouth, then you know
you're not going to be the MVP.

00:36:20.144 --> 00:36:22.413
- Yeah, I'm ready to run through a wall.

00:36:22.413 --> 00:36:27.084
Question, though, because a word
that keeps being said a lot is "doubt."

00:36:28.052 --> 00:36:29.854
Very clear. You felt like people

00:36:29.854 --> 00:36:33.457
really were doubting you before you
made this move here to Chicago.

00:36:33.858 --> 00:36:36.294
Why do you think people doubted you?

00:36:36.360 --> 00:36:38.729
- I don't know. I wish, I wish…

00:36:39.597 --> 00:36:40.598
I wish with the doubt

00:36:40.598 --> 00:36:43.401
they explained the doubt,
because I didn't understand it.

00:36:43.434 --> 00:36:46.971
You know, and I think for me
 and I always try to be a motivation

00:36:47.004 --> 00:36:50.308
and inspiration for people who always feel
doubted in whatever field it is.

00:36:50.308 --> 00:36:51.509
You know what I mean?

00:36:51.509 --> 00:36:53.844
People don't realize,
like, the way I grew up,

00:36:53.844 --> 00:36:56.280
I grew up in Compton, California,

00:36:56.280 --> 00:36:58.883
all you heard was doubt and negativity.

00:36:58.883 --> 00:37:01.118
You know, and I seen
so many people give in to it

00:37:02.587 --> 00:37:05.223
so easy, and I always took offense

00:37:05.256 --> 00:37:09.961
to that my whole life. You know,
so it was always like a trigger for me,

00:37:10.061 --> 00:37:14.465
that's bigger than just basketball.
You know, like, I had to fight

00:37:14.465 --> 00:37:17.802
against the doubt with so many things.
So many things in life.

00:37:17.802 --> 00:37:21.272
And it was it was always on me to be like,
"Nah, damn that doubt.

00:37:21.572 --> 00:37:24.508
You say I can't do it? Watch.
I'mma do it just because of that.
- Yeah.

00:37:24.875 --> 00:37:27.945
You know what I mean? And I carry
 that same mentality to this day.

00:37:28.246 --> 00:37:32.183
And I always tell people,
like, when you hear a naysayer, anybody,

00:37:32.183 --> 00:37:36.621
any walk of life, don't
one second feed into that,

00:37:36.654 --> 00:37:40.291
because you lose as soon as
you think that way.

00:37:40.524 --> 00:37:44.228
You know, and for me, when I hear that
 I'm gonna take on a challenge

00:37:44.228 --> 00:37:47.898
as best as I can every single
way. It pisses me off

00:37:47.932 --> 00:37:50.935
because who are you
to speak on me with anything?

00:37:51.402 --> 00:37:52.036
You know what I mean?
- Mmhmm

00:37:52.036 --> 00:37:54.805
- Like, I just, you know, I hate it.

00:37:54.972 --> 00:37:57.041
- Yeah. Can you give me an example?

00:37:57.041 --> 00:37:58.876
You said, "I've been
fighting doubt for my whole life."

00:37:59.977 --> 00:38:01.712
- Um, I mean, I remember

00:38:02.713 --> 00:38:03.447
dealing with people.

00:38:03.447 --> 00:38:06.250
I remember growing up, you hear

00:38:06.751 --> 00:38:09.987
people across the street
saying—it could be a group of kids,

00:38:10.054 --> 00:38:12.490
when I was a kid, it was a group of us
running around saying,

00:38:13.391 --> 00:38:14.425
"None of ya'll ain't gonna be nothing

00:38:14.425 --> 00:38:16.327
anyway, ya'll need to take your ass in the house."

00:38:16.327 --> 00:38:18.729
You know I was like, "What you mean
I ain't gonna be nothing?"
- Yeah.

00:38:19.597 --> 00:38:21.666
- You know what I mean? It's like.

00:38:21.899 --> 00:38:24.535
Just because of that, I'mma show
you I'mma  be something,

00:38:24.535 --> 00:38:28.739
you know what I mean? And sometimes
that's all the motivation you need

00:38:29.807 --> 00:38:31.876
growing up where I grew up at,
you know what I mean?

00:38:31.876 --> 00:38:34.345
But you heard it so consistently.
You know, I heard teachers

00:38:34.345 --> 00:38:36.280
tell students saying,
"You ain't gonna be nothing.

00:38:36.280 --> 00:38:37.348
You ain't even gonna graduate."

00:38:38.883 --> 00:38:40.618
What? Like, you know what I mean?
- That's wild.

00:38:40.618 --> 00:38:42.720
- Like, you know what I mean? So when
you hear those things, you

00:38:45.022 --> 00:38:48.092
take it personally, you know,
 and sometimes that's the push you need.

00:38:48.092 --> 00:38:51.128
But when you consistently
hear it over and over, it's like, "No,

00:38:51.128 --> 00:38:52.396
all right, I'mma take on this challenge."

00:38:52.396 --> 00:38:56.100
- Yeah, it's funny how life works, though,
 because when you think about that,

00:38:56.367 --> 00:38:57.702
there's probably a really good case to be

00:38:57.702 --> 00:39:01.205
made that if you never heard any of that,
your life could've turned out differently.
- For sure.

00:39:01.472 --> 00:39:02.640
No question.
- You know?
- No question.

00:39:02.640 --> 00:39:06.510
No question. So is it works
different for everybody,

00:39:06.544 --> 00:39:09.580
you know what I mean? For me, like,
 you know, I needed it.

00:39:09.580 --> 00:39:12.183
You know, that was my push.

00:39:12.249 --> 00:39:14.852
You know, it's like, "You said,
 I can't do something?

00:39:15.286 --> 00:39:19.790
Well, all right, I'm 'bout to try, and
I'mma keep trying until I get good at it."

00:39:20.024 --> 00:39:22.193
- Mmhmm.
- And next thing you know, I'm good at it.

00:39:22.593 --> 00:39:25.296
Now what? Then they find something else.

00:39:25.496 --> 00:39:28.799
Now what? You know,
you just keep meeting it.

00:39:28.799 --> 00:39:32.803
That's that was my positive
approach to negativity.

00:39:32.903 --> 00:39:34.972
- Yeah, I mean, and that's
probably the best way to do it,

00:39:34.972 --> 00:39:36.941
because there's always gonna
be negativity around you.
- For sure.

00:39:36.941 --> 00:39:39.377
- It's more so just how are
you going to channel this

00:39:39.410 --> 00:39:42.413
to better yourself and better your career
and what you're doing?
- Yup.

00:39:42.446 --> 00:39:46.717
- So I love that. I have two reports
 I wanna ask you about,
- Mmhmm.

00:39:46.717 --> 00:39:48.619
- you tell me if they were true.
- Talk to me.

00:39:49.754 --> 00:39:52.323
- You were gonna take a pay
cut to join the Lakers.

00:39:53.457 --> 00:39:55.526
- I never understood what
was a pay cut, though.

00:39:56.160 --> 00:39:59.830
- Cuz anything really technically could've been—
- Yeah, anything—I could've took a dollar less.

00:39:59.864 --> 00:40:00.731
That's a pay cut.
- Yeah.

00:40:00.731 --> 00:40:04.001
- You know what I mean?
- But for the sake of the report—
- Yeah.

00:40:04.101 --> 00:40:08.305
- Were you going to take a substantial pay
cut to be able to play for the Lakers?

00:40:08.372 --> 00:40:12.109
- Substantial? I wouldn't say substantial.
- But a hefty—
- A pay cut.

00:40:12.209 --> 00:40:14.111
- A cut.
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.
- OK.

00:40:14.111 --> 00:40:16.046
- It was a, it was a—

00:40:16.046 --> 00:40:17.014
it was an option.

00:40:17.014 --> 00:40:22.386
- Yeah.
- It was, it was a, it
was a real realistic option.

00:40:22.420 --> 00:40:25.389
- OK. And obviously things worked out the way they were supposed to.
- Yeah, it worked out.

00:40:25.389 --> 00:40:27.958
- You're with the Bulls, and you're great.
But explain to me why you say

00:40:27.958 --> 00:40:31.195
it was an option and just—
- One, I wanted to go home.

00:40:33.030 --> 00:40:35.032
That was an opportunity for me to go home.

00:40:35.032 --> 00:40:38.803
You know, you don't get that
opportunity often.

00:40:39.103 --> 00:40:40.571
You know, I got it

00:40:40.838 --> 00:40:43.307
before, but I decided to stay in Toronto.

00:40:46.577 --> 00:40:49.246
It was an option, you know?
Have a, have

00:40:50.047 --> 00:40:52.817
the opportunity to play with
one of the greatest players of all time.

00:40:53.551 --> 00:40:56.554
So why not? It's hard

00:40:56.554 --> 00:41:00.858
to turn that down, you know?
And the want to compete for a championship

00:41:01.859 --> 00:41:05.396
in your hometown.
Like, all those elements just had lined up

00:41:06.063 --> 00:41:11.869
to allow me to consider
a realistic pay cut.

00:41:12.169 --> 00:41:14.271
- [Laughing] OK, so realistic pay cut.
- Yeah.

00:41:14.305 --> 00:41:16.307
- OK, fair. Makes sense.

00:41:16.307 --> 00:41:18.709
Another talk—or, another report

00:41:18.709 --> 00:41:22.980
I remember seeing, that you and Kyle
may be talking about teaming up.

00:41:22.980 --> 00:41:26.250
I don't know how—
- Yeah, yeah.
- Is this true?
- That's true. We talked about it.

00:41:26.450 --> 00:41:27.451
- OK. Walk me through it.

00:41:27.451 --> 00:41:31.889
- No, we had talked about teaming up.

00:41:32.823 --> 00:41:35.059
What was our options?

00:41:35.726 --> 00:41:38.929
How realistic could certain options be?

00:41:39.797 --> 00:41:42.600
What made sense for him,
what made sense for me.

00:41:42.600 --> 00:41:46.270
And nothing just ultimately aligned
in the right way

00:41:46.403 --> 00:41:51.742
where it could have benefited both of us
in a way, you know, we both needed.

00:41:51.876 --> 00:41:55.379
We wanted it too for us to play together,
but it definitely was a talk.

00:41:55.679 --> 00:41:56.647
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:41:56.647 --> 00:41:58.315
- Yeah, were you close? It just—

00:41:58.315 --> 00:42:01.385
- Ah, I wouldn't say it was close,
but it was talked about.
- OK.

00:42:01.519 --> 00:42:03.153
- It was talked about.
- How is Kyle?

00:42:03.153 --> 00:42:05.322
- He's good. He's good.
- Kyle's so funny.

00:42:05.322 --> 00:42:08.359
You're—y'all's friendship
is so interesting to me because, and

00:42:08.392 --> 00:42:12.229
I mean this with so much respect
and love in my heart,

00:42:12.463 --> 00:42:14.098
Kyle really is an asshole.

00:42:14.098 --> 00:42:16.734
- No, for sure.
- Like, so—when you know him, he's so nice.

00:42:16.834 --> 00:42:20.137
- Yeah, I've been on record saying it.
- Yeah. He's great.

00:42:20.371 --> 00:42:23.774
He has always been very kind to me,
but he is an asshole to people who

00:42:24.975 --> 00:42:29.179
maybe don't know him. So
did you always like Kyle?

00:42:29.580 --> 00:42:32.716
- No.
- OK.
- No, and he know it,
 and everybody around

00:42:32.716 --> 00:42:36.554
know it. Our first year together—
because anybody that know me,

00:42:36.554 --> 00:42:41.492
I'm such a, I'm a chill person,
stay to myself.

00:42:41.792 --> 00:42:45.896
But I do have a certain side to me
that I just never wanna bring out.

00:42:46.263 --> 00:42:48.899
And Kyle was an asshole to where

00:42:48.899 --> 00:42:52.536
I knew if I was around him long enough,
he would bring that other side out.

00:42:52.937 --> 00:42:55.606
So I just stayed away from him
cuz I didn't know him.

00:42:55.806 --> 00:42:59.276
I didn't know him personally
to understand him.

00:43:00.744 --> 00:43:02.580
But as time went on,

00:43:02.580 --> 00:43:07.818
and we just ended up in certain situations
together in the game, off the court,

00:43:08.185 --> 00:43:11.488
you know, you start to learn them,
understand them more.

00:43:11.922 --> 00:43:13.624
It was like, "Yo, this is my dawg."

00:43:13.624 --> 00:43:16.594
Like, I'll run through 10 brick
walls for him in a heartbeat—
- Yeah.

00:43:16.627 --> 00:43:19.363
- You know what I mean? And it's
just crazy how it worked.

00:43:20.064 --> 00:43:22.399
You know, when they say opposites attract,

00:43:22.433 --> 00:43:24.868
that's, you know, that's,
you know, ultimately what happened.

00:43:24.969 --> 00:43:27.438
But he's definitely an asshole.

00:43:27.438 --> 00:43:29.907
But in that same token, he's one of the

00:43:30.874 --> 00:43:33.243
best people to have as a friend.
- Totally.

00:43:33.577 --> 00:43:37.648
- No question. Like I, no matter what
I can call him any time of the night.

00:43:38.082 --> 00:43:43.187
If I needed $5 million, he'd, "OK,
I'll send it in the morning,"

00:43:43.954 --> 00:43:48.092
you know what I mean?
- Totally.
- Just—not saying I'd ask him for that, but I'm
just saying, that's just what

00:43:48.092 --> 00:43:51.562
type of friend he is when he really care
and love for somebody, you know what I mean?

00:43:51.562 --> 00:43:54.632
He would give his last
just to make sure you straight, so.

00:43:54.632 --> 00:43:58.669
- Yeah.
- You know, on top of him being an asshole,
he's one of the best friends you'll have.

00:43:58.669 --> 00:44:00.304
- Yes, he's a great human.

00:44:00.304 --> 00:44:03.741
And it's interesting
because you all—a lot of guys in the league,

00:44:03.741 --> 00:44:07.211
you know, they're like, "Oh, this is my dude.
This is my best friend." Y'all are really

00:44:08.278 --> 00:44:10.848
best friends for life forever.

00:44:10.881 --> 00:44:11.548
- Yeah, that's my dawg.

00:44:11.548 --> 00:44:14.151
- Yeah. And I don't think
we see that all the time.

00:44:14.184 --> 00:44:16.954
- Nah, because this—
and it's crazy because, like,

00:44:18.022 --> 00:44:21.392
nobody really see us outside of,
you know, basketball.

00:44:21.392 --> 00:44:23.560
But, like, everything we do

00:44:23.861 --> 00:44:27.097
outside of basketball
is the most normal stuff in the world.

00:44:27.731 --> 00:44:31.268
We will argue over
the most craziest situation

00:44:31.268 --> 00:44:33.771
that ain't got nothin' to do with sports.
Like, you know, that's really

00:44:34.304 --> 00:44:35.305
real life my friend.

00:44:35.305 --> 00:44:38.776
Like, once—I'm pretty sure
when we 50, 60 years old,

00:44:38.809 --> 00:44:41.178
we're gonna be sitting on the porch
telling stories.

00:44:41.178 --> 00:44:43.647
And I'mma be saying
he lied about something and vice versa.

00:44:43.647 --> 00:44:46.884
You know what I mean? Like,
that's us. That's gonna be us.
- Yeah.

00:44:46.917 --> 00:44:50.287
No, I love a beautiful friendship,
so that's amazing.

00:44:50.287 --> 00:44:52.156
I hope you guys get to see
each other in the offseason and stuff.

00:44:52.156 --> 00:44:53.490
- Yeah, yeah, we do.
- OK.

00:44:53.490 --> 00:44:55.592
- But, like you said,

00:44:55.592 --> 00:44:58.195
people don't get to see that side.
They see when we play against each other.

00:44:58.195 --> 00:45:00.431
But whenever he come to L.A.,

00:45:00.431 --> 00:45:04.501
we spend a lot of time together
when people don't see.

00:45:04.501 --> 00:45:08.739
- Yeah, no, I see that. I love that.
Y'all are everybody's favorite

00:45:08.806 --> 00:45:11.108
NBA friendship. Totally.
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:45:11.909 --> 00:45:14.845
- I definitely want to make sure
 that I give you my love

00:45:14.845 --> 00:45:18.816
and my condolences about your dad, Frank,
 who unfortunately passed.
- Thank you, thank you.

00:45:19.817 --> 00:45:23.420
- I read how you call him Big Dog,
 and I love that nickname.
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:45:23.620 --> 00:45:26.023
- Tell me why you call him that.

00:45:26.123 --> 00:45:27.291
- Um...it really started from my cousin,

00:45:27.291 --> 00:45:29.059
my other cousin that's here,

00:45:29.059 --> 00:45:32.763
just always called him Big Dog.
Like, he was always the Big Dog.

00:45:32.930 --> 00:45:37.000
My dad was the boss
 of all bosses.

00:45:37.000 --> 00:45:40.871
Like, when I got to the league, he always
 said like, "I'm still the boss.

00:45:40.904 --> 00:45:42.806
I don't care how much money you've got."

00:45:42.806 --> 00:45:46.343
So it was always like, "All right.
My bad, Big Dog. You got it." You know?

00:45:46.343 --> 00:45:49.747
And he just always carried himself in
 being a protector

00:45:50.380 --> 00:45:55.419
of the whole family, you know? And that's
 where the term Big Dog comes from. Of him

00:45:55.419 --> 00:45:58.422
just, you know, kind of
 just always being the enforcer,

00:45:58.722 --> 00:46:02.359
you know, in the most positive
 and loving way, as much as he could.

00:46:02.359 --> 00:46:04.862
And, you know, everybody loved my dad.

00:46:04.895 --> 00:46:07.765
So, you know, that's
 kind of where it stemmed from.

00:46:07.765 --> 00:46:11.335
But I've gotta give that credit to my,
 my older cousin.

00:46:11.568 --> 00:46:13.971
He gave him that nickname
and we all just stuck with it.

00:46:14.171 --> 00:46:15.038
- That's great.

00:46:15.038 --> 00:46:19.176
What parts of your dad
do you see in you?

00:46:20.611 --> 00:46:26.083
- Um...relentless.
I done seen him go through

00:46:26.784 --> 00:46:29.019
everything, you know, and not

00:46:29.686 --> 00:46:33.056
not break, not fold,
not give up, not complain.

00:46:33.657 --> 00:46:38.695
And that's one trait, to this day,
 you know, even after he passed that,

00:46:39.062 --> 00:46:41.765
I revealed...that I realized that

00:46:41.765 --> 00:46:45.202
I had that much in me from him,

00:46:45.669 --> 00:46:49.273
you know? And it was
 it was crazy because it was like,

00:46:49.840 --> 00:46:52.843
you know, even when he passed,
it's like, it's all right.

00:46:52.876 --> 00:46:56.413
Everything you taught me
 for these 31 years

00:46:57.281 --> 00:47:00.184
helped me through this,
 through this tragedy.

00:47:00.217 --> 00:47:01.051
You know what I mean?
- Mm-hmm.

00:47:01.051 --> 00:47:04.188
- And I felt that learning everything
that I went through with my dad

00:47:04.188 --> 00:47:05.189
watching him, everything he taught me

00:47:05.189 --> 00:47:09.660
just everything, you know,
and I realize that more than
- Yeah.

00:47:09.726 --> 00:47:11.195
anything after I lost him.

00:47:11.195 --> 00:47:13.831
- Like in some ways, his life prepared you for his death.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:47:13.831 --> 00:47:17.668
- You know, I mean, it's crazy that you
 say that

00:47:17.668 --> 00:47:18.969
because that's exactly what it was.

00:47:18.969 --> 00:47:24.208
- Yeah. I read you saying that you felt
 you hadn't dealt with it personally yet.

00:47:24.241 --> 00:47:26.877
Like, you didn't really get that moment to grieve yet.
- Mm-hmm.

00:47:26.877 --> 00:47:29.546
- But when it comes, you were gonna let it hit you.
- Yeah.

00:47:30.147 --> 00:47:33.150
- Have you been able to deal with it?
- Nah, I still haven't.

00:47:33.150 --> 00:47:37.020
And it's the most eerie thing for me, because um,

00:47:39.656 --> 00:47:43.427
like, you know, I don't know when it's gonna come,
how it's gonna come.

00:47:43.594 --> 00:47:46.463
You know, it's just...

00:47:46.530 --> 00:47:47.631
I got him tatted on my arm

00:47:47.631 --> 00:47:51.435
because I feel like I carry him
 everywhere I go, you know, and it's

00:47:51.501 --> 00:47:52.436
one of those things to where

00:47:52.436 --> 00:47:56.540
it's like, I'm just waiting for him
 to pop off my arm and tell me something.

00:47:56.573 --> 00:47:57.641
You know what I mean? For me

00:47:57.641 --> 00:48:00.277
to just like, let it all out, you know?

00:48:00.277 --> 00:48:05.182
But it's something that maybe it's just
 the strength that I've gotten from him

00:48:05.582 --> 00:48:07.284
allow me to just keep pushing.

00:48:07.284 --> 00:48:10.187
But at some point, I know

00:48:10.254 --> 00:48:13.257
it'll get to me whenever
 it need to get to me

00:48:13.724 --> 00:48:16.493
in the right time.
- Yeah, my mom,

00:48:17.527 --> 00:48:19.496
when she was younger, her brother passed

00:48:19.496 --> 00:48:23.600
and then both of her parents passed
 within a year-and-a-half of each other.

00:48:24.067 --> 00:48:27.104
And one thing that she has taught me
 a lot about grief

00:48:27.104 --> 00:48:29.673
is she's like, "Grief really
 isn't a thing that goes away."

00:48:30.107 --> 00:48:33.143
She's like, "When people
 haven't dealt with big losses,

00:48:33.477 --> 00:48:36.380
they feel like, you know, through time

00:48:36.380 --> 00:48:38.315
it feels better," you know?

00:48:38.315 --> 00:48:41.084
She's like, "It's more that
 like, through time, it feels more real.

00:48:41.451 --> 00:48:45.389
And you become accustomed
 to living in that, that realness of it."
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:48:45.422 --> 00:48:48.292
- Have you learned that about grief?
- For sure.

00:48:48.325 --> 00:48:50.294
I learned, and it sucks,

00:48:50.294 --> 00:48:55.299
because just like what you said,
 I've learned it so early on,

00:48:55.499 --> 00:48:58.602
you know, that, you know, not necessarily

00:48:58.602 --> 00:48:59.469
it made me numb to it,

00:48:59.469 --> 00:49:03.607
it just made me understand
 the value of life

00:49:03.740 --> 00:49:06.410
when you say you love
 somebody. You know, um,

00:49:07.311 --> 00:49:11.648
I've lost countless uncles
 at a young age to gun violence.

00:49:11.648 --> 00:49:14.418
I lost, at 16, lost my grandmother.

00:49:14.418 --> 00:49:16.420
Six days later, I lost my grandfather.

00:49:16.453 --> 00:49:21.024
You know, so from that same token
 what you just said, you learn how to

00:49:22.559 --> 00:49:25.862
deal with it in the moment
 because it never, it never goes away.

00:49:26.063 --> 00:49:27.531
You know, you have your good,

00:49:27.531 --> 00:49:30.968
your bad days. But you learn,
 you learn to deal with and you learn,

00:49:30.968 --> 00:49:34.805
at the same token,
to appreciate your loved ones even more.

00:49:35.005 --> 00:49:39.309
You know? There's two things that
I've kind of learned throughout my whole life.

00:49:39.309 --> 00:49:43.013
That's why I always give so much respect to people

00:49:43.347 --> 00:49:47.351
when I come across people,
 you know what I mean? It's like, I'm always be—
- Yeah. Mm-hmm.

00:49:47.551 --> 00:49:52.389
- Giving you the utmost respect
 because I want you to feel my energy.

00:49:52.656 --> 00:49:55.359
You know what I mean? Because nothing
 is never promised, you know?
- 100 percent. Yeah.

00:49:55.926 --> 00:49:57.594
- I lost so much before

00:49:57.594 --> 00:50:01.431
I could even understand
what death was, you know what I mean?

00:50:01.465 --> 00:50:04.901
So that's something I always carry
when I'm always meeting

00:50:04.901 --> 00:50:07.104
people or I'm always around people is,

00:50:07.637 --> 00:50:11.608
you know, you try to give off that lasting
impression because I would hope me

00:50:11.641 --> 00:50:14.644
being the way I am will help you
be like that to somebody else.
- Yeah.

00:50:14.678 --> 00:50:17.147
- And so that's just always been my mentality.

00:50:17.347 --> 00:50:21.151
- That's good. And honestly, that was what
I thought about when I saw the news.

00:50:21.151 --> 00:50:23.420
Cuz, you remember, we did, like, last summer…

00:50:23.720 --> 00:50:25.088
yeah, like, last summer—

00:50:26.490 --> 00:50:29.693
we talked about—kind of during George
Floyd, we did that interview.
- Yeah. Yeah, yeah, yeah,

00:50:30.093 --> 00:50:34.197
- And one thing you discussed
was feeling very desensitized to death—
- Yeah.

00:50:34.531 --> 00:50:35.799
- In a lot of ways.

00:50:35.799 --> 00:50:37.567
How it has—like, you were always
going to funerals

00:50:37.567 --> 00:50:39.736
when you were younger,
and it just kind of started to be

00:50:39.736 --> 00:50:42.472
something that felt like very much
so a part of your life.

00:50:43.673 --> 00:50:46.143
So I'm really glad to hear that
you are, you know,

00:50:46.176 --> 00:50:49.279
coping really well with it—
- Yeah.
- Day to day and pushing through.

00:50:49.279 --> 00:50:53.617
And I know you said you really were
looking for kind of this peace with it.

00:50:53.650 --> 00:50:56.720
When do you feel like
you'll find that peace?

00:50:56.720 --> 00:50:57.854
Have you come close?

00:50:57.854 --> 00:50:59.122
- Um, I'm not sure.

00:50:59.122 --> 00:51:02.125
I really wish I had an answer to give.

00:51:02.159 --> 00:51:05.262
Not just for myself. Just for anybody
who dealin' with any type of tragedy.

00:51:06.430 --> 00:51:09.900
And I just—for me,
I just realize, like, life is

00:51:11.001 --> 00:51:15.105
one of those things you realize
you just will never have the full answer to,

00:51:15.338 --> 00:51:20.544
you know? You just gotta deal with it
in real time with real emotions,

00:51:20.710 --> 00:51:25.882
you know? And I don't run from 'em,
you know? For a lotta my years,

00:51:25.882 --> 00:51:31.621
when I was young, I ran from a lotta,
a lotta my tragedies because I felt like,

00:51:32.522 --> 00:51:35.625
well, I was just running from it
cuz I don't wanna be next, you know?
- Mmhmm.

00:51:35.926 --> 00:51:39.262
- And that's, you know,
especially for me, losing so many friends

00:51:39.262 --> 00:51:44.234
so, so early, it was like,
you know, I didn't wanna deal with it

00:51:44.568 --> 00:51:47.771
because I just didn't wanna
deal with it, you know? It's like—
- Yeah.

00:51:48.905 --> 00:51:51.141
- Cuz I always felt like it never was gonna stop.
- Mmhmm.

00:51:51.641 --> 00:51:55.045
- But, you know, it's
just one of those things like,

00:51:55.412 --> 00:51:57.681
you know, you gotta deal with it.
Like, we human at the end of the day.

00:51:57.981 --> 00:51:59.149
- Yeah.
- We got emotions.

00:51:59.149 --> 00:52:02.085
You gotta feel 'em, you know?
And that's what, that's what makes you

00:52:02.319 --> 00:52:07.757
you is when you, you know, you indulge
in emotion. Because there's nothing realer than

00:52:07.791 --> 00:52:11.294
emotion, you know? You can't see it, but
you feel it more than anything, you know what I mean?

00:52:11.361 --> 00:52:14.498
- Yeah.
- Whether if it's happy, sad, you gotta feel it.

00:52:14.865 --> 00:52:16.666
You gotta feel it. And for me,

00:52:16.666 --> 00:52:18.668
when I do feel those things, I

00:52:19.269 --> 00:52:22.506
indulge in it because that's
the only thing that makes me feel human.

00:52:22.539 --> 00:52:25.809
- Yeah. It's like you have to feel it,
and you have to be able to talk about it

00:52:25.809 --> 00:52:29.713
too. Even just when I think about the, like,
the expectations

00:52:29.713 --> 00:52:31.615
that society and culture puts on people,

00:52:31.615 --> 00:52:35.452
but men specifically—
- Yeah.
- Like, feeling like they cannot say

00:52:35.452 --> 00:52:39.022
if they're sad or hurt or need help.
- Yeah.

00:52:39.222 --> 00:52:39.923
- And that's one of the things

00:52:39.923 --> 00:52:43.760
everybody says they give you a big credit
for just really talking about

00:52:43.960 --> 00:52:47.297
how you felt. And I know we discussed this
last time, that Kevin Love saying

00:52:47.564 --> 00:52:51.902
you are the entire reason
he felt like he could do that, too.
- Yeah.

00:52:52.235 --> 00:52:57.807
- Do you see, like, what you kind of
are to people cuz you're so open?

00:52:58.475 --> 00:53:01.111
- Yeah, but I look towards people…

00:53:03.313 --> 00:53:05.115
rather they know it—

00:53:05.482 --> 00:53:07.684
I look towards other people's stories

00:53:07.684 --> 00:53:10.520
more than anything,
because that's what keeps me going.

00:53:11.188 --> 00:53:11.821
You know what I mean?

00:53:11.821 --> 00:53:13.990
Like, people may look at me
 like, "Oh, you did—"

00:53:13.990 --> 00:53:17.394
But a lotta times, even players,
I don't know, even in sports

00:53:18.061 --> 00:53:20.830
I don't know, not even in
sports, in the entertainment world.

00:53:20.830 --> 00:53:25.168
Anyone who has a story for me,
I indulge in because

00:53:26.536 --> 00:53:32.108
I gain so much empathy
and inspiration from that because

00:53:32.108 --> 00:53:36.313
we carry so much that people don't see,
you know what I mean? Like, I

00:53:36.713 --> 00:53:40.083
always look at people on TV and be like,

00:53:40.083 --> 00:53:43.920
"I wonder how they do this,"
whether if it's a comedian or something.

00:53:44.187 --> 00:53:46.890
Like, "How'd you do this? Because you have a life
outside of this."

00:53:47.023 --> 00:53:47.958
You know what I mean?
- Mmhmm.

00:53:47.958 --> 00:53:48.825
- Like, what's your story?

00:53:48.825 --> 00:53:51.928
So when I can find stories—
like Jim Carrey, for

00:53:52.462 --> 00:53:53.430
one. That's one person I

00:53:53.430 --> 00:53:55.665
always—if he do an interview,

00:53:55.665 --> 00:54:01.738
If he speak so much stuff, I indulge
in him because his—the way he speaks on

00:54:03.073 --> 00:54:04.507
humanity and being

00:54:04.507 --> 00:54:08.245
human and empathy, depression
and so much stuff,

00:54:08.678 --> 00:54:10.447
he paints a picture with it.

00:54:10.447 --> 00:54:12.249
You know what I mean?
It's the most incredible thing,

00:54:12.249 --> 00:54:16.119
and I find so much inspiration in it
because he realized something

00:54:16.720 --> 00:54:19.089
through everything he been through.

00:54:19.589 --> 00:54:20.557
And he speaks on it.

00:54:20.557 --> 00:54:21.591
You know what I mean?
- Mmhmm.

00:54:21.591 --> 00:54:23.426
- And to me, that's amazing

00:54:23.426 --> 00:54:27.597
when anybody, any walk of life, does that.
And I find inspiration and motivation

00:54:27.597 --> 00:54:30.700
to keep going with that
because we all got a story.

00:54:30.734 --> 00:54:34.371
- Yeah.
- We all got a story. So it's just crazy.

00:54:34.437 --> 00:54:38.141
But when—if anybody look at me
that way, I hope I help

00:54:38.908 --> 00:54:41.611
in any type of way
when I, whenever I speak out,

00:54:41.978 --> 00:54:45.582
I'm still trying to learn
to get my words out the best I can

00:54:45.582 --> 00:54:48.485
to express my emotions and my feelings
whenever I'm going through something

00:54:49.552 --> 00:54:51.888
the best way I can to help people out.

00:54:52.322 --> 00:54:55.792
But, you know, I lean on everything
and everybody as much as I can,

00:54:55.925 --> 00:54:58.128
whether people know it or not.
- No, that's great.

00:54:58.128 --> 00:55:01.231
And you really are a testament
to, like, people don't really know

00:55:01.231 --> 00:55:03.300
the full scope of anybody.
Cuz I actually didn't know

00:55:03.300 --> 00:55:09.406
until I was prepping for this that that
season you were flying to see your dad.

00:55:09.506 --> 00:55:13.276
- Yeah. I don't think I never—
 I never talked about it. For three—

00:55:13.310 --> 00:55:19.082
even in Toronto.
My dad got sick Christmas 2018.

00:55:20.450 --> 00:55:23.019
My last year in Toronto,
my dad got sick, went in the hospital.

00:55:23.019 --> 00:55:25.455
I flew home a couple days
before Christmas,

00:55:26.823 --> 00:55:29.893
sat with my dad. And he was, he was in the hospital

00:55:30.493 --> 00:55:33.596
for the next—in and out of the hospital
the next three years after that.

00:55:33.596 --> 00:55:34.931
So in three years,

00:55:36.132 --> 00:55:39.869
I was—I done flew from Boston
to L.A., L.A. to Toronto

00:55:40.303 --> 00:55:43.340
by myself, played in the game.
Did the same thing in San Antonio.

00:55:43.807 --> 00:55:44.341
But nobody

00:55:44.341 --> 00:55:46.810
never knew. It was times I flew in
day of the game and played,

00:55:47.811 --> 00:55:51.681
from seeing my dad.
- So, leave L.A.,

00:55:51.881 --> 00:55:55.652
fly in game day and play that night.
- Play that night.

00:55:55.785 --> 00:55:58.722
I did it so many times, countless times.
I never talked about it.

00:55:58.988 --> 00:56:00.056
Never talked about it.

00:56:00.056 --> 00:56:02.125
- I mean, how difficult was that

00:56:02.125 --> 00:56:04.961
when you were making these trips
and then having to go to work?
- I mean,

00:56:05.061 --> 00:56:07.564
it was, it was, it was the most—

00:56:07.564 --> 00:56:11.768
it was probably the hardest thing
in my life to do because, you know,

00:56:11.768 --> 00:56:16.773
you have so much idle
time of sitting on a plane by yourself,

00:56:18.108 --> 00:56:20.677
dealing with the emotional,

00:56:20.677 --> 00:56:25.882
like, trauma of seeing your dad—
seeing the person, that only person in life

00:56:25.882 --> 00:56:28.952
I ever feared,
you know, down and out,

00:56:30.120 --> 00:56:32.689
to having to go play
in front of 20,000 people.

00:56:33.256 --> 00:56:36.159
You know, it was like
the emotional roller coaster

00:56:36.159 --> 00:56:38.962
that I dealt with for,
 you know, three years was

00:56:39.462 --> 00:56:42.899
one of the toughest things ever
because, one. I never wanted to be

00:56:42.899 --> 00:56:47.704
a distraction to my teammates,
to the game or nothing.

00:56:47.704 --> 00:56:49.639
So, you know, I dealt with everything

00:56:49.639 --> 00:56:51.775
I dealt with personally,
and I didn't wanna be loud about it.

00:56:51.775 --> 00:56:55.412
I didn't want nobody necessarily
to feel sorry for me and everything, but

00:56:55.412 --> 00:56:59.382
I also wanted to be there for my teammates
and my family, you know what I mean?

00:56:59.382 --> 00:57:02.685
So to carry those emotions consistently,

00:57:03.253 --> 00:57:06.322
you know, behind the scenes,
and nobody knew, you know, it was, it was,

00:57:06.823 --> 00:57:08.224
it was tough. It was tough.

00:57:08.224 --> 00:57:12.695
Like, even when I lost my dad,
I was in a hotel in Charlotte.

00:57:12.962 --> 00:57:16.266
Like, just finished playing a game. Fly home,

00:57:16.699 --> 00:57:20.303
you know, home a couple of days,
and I flew back with the team, played

00:57:20.303 --> 00:57:24.140
in a game. Fly back home, go to his funeral
leave for shootaround and go play.

00:57:24.441 --> 00:57:27.243
You know, it was like
all those emotions to where—

00:57:27.977 --> 00:57:30.780
what really made it
hard, you know, like I said,

00:57:30.780 --> 00:57:33.850
I'm sitting on a plane
by myself for hours, like,

00:57:35.018 --> 00:57:37.854
you know, with this
on your mind, trying to figure out how,

00:57:37.887 --> 00:57:41.458
all right, how you gonna balance yourself
out to be able to play tonight,

00:57:41.724 --> 00:57:45.562
you know?
- Yeah.
- But I needed to play. Like I said, my teammates, guys

00:57:45.562 --> 00:57:48.798
I played with knew what I was going through,
and they was always there for me,

00:57:48.798 --> 00:57:50.266
and they made it easy for me.

00:57:50.266 --> 00:57:55.104
And that's why I always felt like I had
to be there for them as well to compete.
- Yeah.

00:57:55.939 --> 00:57:58.074
- So, yeah, that was for three years.

00:57:58.074 --> 00:58:01.644
- Yeah, because I mean, on a plane,
like, literally all you can do is think.

00:58:01.911 --> 00:58:06.483
- Yeah.
- Like, you're just in your head.
- In your head the whole time.
- The entire time.
- The entire time.

00:58:06.749 --> 00:58:08.751
- And I would assume
when you were playing basketball,

00:58:08.751 --> 00:58:11.488
you were also thinking
about everything too.

00:58:11.955 --> 00:58:14.090
- For me, basketball was my escape.

00:58:14.390 --> 00:58:19.262
It's always been my escape from everything.
Like my whole entire life,

00:58:19.295 --> 00:58:21.664
that was always my thing. Like,

00:58:21.664 --> 00:58:26.436
I needed to go play basketball, but it was,
it was hard because, you know, those two

00:58:26.436 --> 00:58:29.739
hours, those two-and-a-half hours you
in between those lines, you feel free.

00:58:30.473 --> 00:58:34.210
Soon as you walk outside those lines, it's
like, "All right, I'm out the matrix now.

00:58:34.210 --> 00:58:38.648
So, like, how am I to deal
with what's next?"

00:58:38.948 --> 00:58:40.683
- Yeah.
- You know, and it's back to it.

00:58:40.683 --> 00:58:44.053
This, for a prime example,
never told anybody this.

00:58:45.455 --> 00:58:48.825
Like, this is why Kyle's
one of my— will forever be my man.

00:58:49.325 --> 00:58:54.130
It was, it was so many games we played in to
where his locker was next to mine,

00:58:54.130 --> 00:58:56.165
where we'd—when we'd come out
the game and sit next

00:58:56.366 --> 00:58:58.268
to each other in the locker room,

00:58:58.268 --> 00:59:00.270
he used to say, he was like,
"Man, back to reality

00:59:00.270 --> 00:59:03.640
now. Do you—what can I—you know I'm here with you."
You know what I mean?

00:59:03.640 --> 00:59:05.575
Because he knew the severity

00:59:05.575 --> 00:59:08.545
and understood what I was going through
more than anybody. So,

00:59:09.712 --> 00:59:12.348
yeah, that's what I was dealing with.
- Wow.

00:59:12.348 --> 00:59:14.517
Oh, my gosh. I am so sorry to hear that.

00:59:15.218 --> 00:59:16.719
But I do wanna give you an opportunity:

00:59:16.719 --> 00:59:19.489
Tell us, what should we know about your dad?

00:59:19.522 --> 00:59:21.357
What did you love the most about your dad?

00:59:23.126 --> 00:59:26.095
- What I loved—everything
I loved about him was everything

00:59:26.095 --> 00:59:28.398
as a kid, you hated about him, you know
what I mean?

00:59:28.431 --> 00:59:31.200
- [Laughing] Yeah.
- He'd push you so hard to where it was like,

00:59:31.234 --> 00:59:33.670
"Man, I don't wanna play basketball anymore.
I don't wanna do this.

00:59:33.670 --> 00:59:35.838
I just wanna go home.
I wanna watch cartoons."

00:59:36.306 --> 00:59:39.275
But he instilled so much in me

00:59:39.409 --> 00:59:43.746
that made me the person and the man
that I am today. My discipline,

00:59:44.981 --> 00:59:46.950
my awareness to

00:59:46.950 --> 00:59:51.421
just life in general. How to read

00:59:51.454 --> 00:59:56.092
and be kind to people.
How to, you know, be the survival—

00:59:56.926 --> 01:00:01.831
like, how to survive
in the toughest environment. Like, everything.

01:00:01.898 --> 01:00:06.803
And besides all that, just being a loving
dad. You know what I mean? He loved—

01:00:06.803 --> 01:00:09.072
he gave me tough love, but he loved,

01:00:09.138 --> 01:00:11.441
he loved the hell out of me every bit, so.

01:00:11.441 --> 01:00:16.613
- Yeah.
- He was just one of those dudes
 that everybody loved. Like, everybody.

01:00:16.779 --> 01:00:19.816
- Yeah. Oh, that's so beautiful.
How old are your kids now?

01:00:20.083 --> 01:00:21.751
- My kids, my oldest

01:00:22.852 --> 01:00:26.623
about to be nine.
- Wow.
- It's, it's—

01:00:27.490 --> 01:00:28.091
- [Laughing] That's wild.

01:00:28.091 --> 01:00:31.094
- See, that's when it start making me feel
old, when you start asking—that's

01:00:31.094 --> 01:00:34.130
what really makes me feel old.
- "Yeah, I got a nine-year-old." Yeah.
- Like [exhaling]—

01:00:35.832 --> 01:00:38.167
it's drivin' me crazy when I sit over
here and think about it.

01:00:38.801 --> 01:00:41.237
- I love it. Well, you know what
I think of now when I think of,

01:00:41.237 --> 01:00:45.141
like. a kid, I think of Steven
Adams picking up—

01:00:45.308 --> 01:00:47.010
- Oh, yeah. Oh, my teammate?

01:00:47.010 --> 01:00:49.545
- Yes!
- Yeah, I told him—

01:00:49.545 --> 01:00:51.280
- Picking him up!
- Yeah, I told him. I said, "Tony, you too big

01:00:51.280 --> 01:00:54.317
to let a grown man pick you up. Your feet
dangling off the ground."

01:00:55.985 --> 01:00:59.355
- [Laughing] It was so bad.
- Yeah.
- Like, literally Steven Adams picked him up,

01:00:59.555 --> 01:01:03.359
like your one-year-old and said,
"All right, get off of my, of my teammate."

01:01:03.559 --> 01:01:07.430
There's no way Tony got back to his—
he had to look at his phone and say,

01:01:07.430 --> 01:01:10.433
"I ain't even gonna look at this tonight,
cuz they killin' me online."

01:01:10.466 --> 01:01:11.868
- Yeah, yeah, they was—

01:01:11.868 --> 01:01:13.970
I was killin' him
in the locker room. Trust me.

01:01:14.904 --> 01:01:16.005
- Oh my gosh, I love it.

01:01:16.005 --> 01:01:22.178
Well, it is always so amazing to see you,
but I do have one more question for you.

01:01:22.178 --> 01:01:25.214
We're having some fans in the app
ask questions, which I'm

01:01:25.214 --> 01:01:28.351
happy about because you know Ayo went to
University of Illinois,

01:01:29.585 --> 01:01:32.989
my alma mater, I-L-L,
You know, big fan of Ayo.

01:01:32.989 --> 01:01:35.458
I saw it before the Bulls saw it, you know?
- Yeah. You did, yeah.

01:01:35.491 --> 01:01:37.560
- What's it like
being a mentor to him?

01:01:37.560 --> 01:01:40.630
- Man, I love it, man. He a hell of a kid, man.

01:01:41.330 --> 01:01:42.932
And I don't even like callin' kids

01:01:42.932 --> 01:01:45.635
"kids" cuz, at the end of the day, you a man, aren't you?

01:01:46.035 --> 01:01:48.638
But he, he's special.
- Mmhmm.

01:01:48.871 --> 01:01:53.776
- He's special. Not just basketball-wise.
That's been showing for itself with

01:01:53.776 --> 01:01:56.512
what he do on the court,
but off the court, man, he a

01:01:56.646 --> 01:02:01.017
hell of a dude. And he's special.
He one of a kind.

01:02:01.017 --> 01:02:04.454
He one of the most inquisitive young kids
that I've ever been around.

01:02:04.487 --> 01:02:07.690
Like, if I tell him somethin',
he will ask 10 questions

01:02:07.690 --> 01:02:10.760
about that one subject.
- Really?

01:02:10.960 --> 01:02:16.399
- But what—but, you know that—
and he sincerely wanna know and learn,

01:02:16.399 --> 01:02:16.966
you know what I mean?

01:02:16.966 --> 01:02:18.301
Like, he'll sit up here

01:02:18.301 --> 01:02:22.271
and ask me about certain players
from 10 years ago. "How was he?"

01:02:22.872 --> 01:02:26.008
So, and stuff like that
just let me know how special he is.

01:02:26.008 --> 01:02:30.246
So it's dope. It's dope to be able to
be any type of mentor towards him in

01:02:30.279 --> 01:02:33.149
any type of way.
- And did you enjoy your time at University of Illinois?

01:02:33.249 --> 01:02:35.384
- I had a great time. Yeah, I had—
- It's a great campus, right?

01:02:35.384 --> 01:02:36.953
- A great campus, yes.
- Yes.

01:02:36.953 --> 01:02:38.454
- And I was, I was—

01:02:38.454 --> 01:02:40.857
it was far. But, yeah,

01:02:40.857 --> 01:02:43.092
It was, it was worth it.

01:02:43.092 --> 01:02:46.629
But just being there, supporting him,
just getting a college atmosphere, just,

01:02:46.729 --> 01:02:49.198
you know, seeing what that was like,
it was dope.

01:02:49.298 --> 01:02:50.566
- Yeah, cuz you were there—

01:02:50.566 --> 01:02:53.903
it was for his jersey retirement, correct?
- Yeah, for his retirement.

01:02:53.903 --> 01:02:57.473
- Which totally, like, speaks to you going to
Champaign to be there for your teammate.

01:02:57.874 --> 01:02:58.941
Such a good vet!

01:02:58.941 --> 01:02:59.609
- Nah, yeah, I had to go.

01:02:59.609 --> 01:03:01.077
I had to text him.

01:03:01.077 --> 01:03:04.113
I had actually texted him
mad that he didn't tell me.

01:03:04.514 --> 01:03:07.250
- Ohh!
- I'm like, "Damn, you can't tell me
about your jersey retirement?"

01:03:07.250 --> 01:03:10.520
He said, "My bad, big brah." I said, "I'll be there."
He was like, "What, for real?!"

01:03:10.887 --> 01:03:12.922
I said, "Yeah, I'll be there." And
I ended up going.

01:03:13.089 --> 01:03:14.891
- Oh, my gosh. Yeah, like, my Illinois

01:03:14.891 --> 01:03:19.061
group chats, they're sending photos, you know,
I'm like, "Wow, DeMar is at State Farm Center."

01:03:19.128 --> 01:03:20.329
I love that. What a guy!

01:03:20.329 --> 01:03:23.099
- Yeah, I had to.
- Didn't—the Lakers missed out, didn't they?

01:03:23.699 --> 01:03:25.902
- Did they?
- Did they?! Hey,

01:03:26.402 --> 01:03:29.572
looks like it. No, you are phenomenal.

01:03:29.739 --> 01:03:31.507
I am so appreciative of you.

01:03:31.507 --> 01:03:34.410
You come on the show whenever I say, "Hey,
do you wanna come on?"

01:03:34.644 --> 01:03:36.512
So, thank you so much for your time.
- Thank you.

01:03:36.512 --> 01:03:40.483
Thanks for, like, giving us a trip
to Chicago cuz I love coming back.

01:03:41.083 --> 01:03:43.953
- Nah, I'm sorry it's seven degrees outside, but—
- Oh, my gosh.

01:03:44.387 --> 01:03:46.656
Yeah, you gotta bundle up.
- Bundle up for sure.

01:03:46.923 --> 01:03:48.324
- Thanks so much, DeMar.
- Thank you.

